[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278375 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ac160c0-9e31-53a2-8e67-6a875cc69c83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{4}}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{x}{x+1}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275726} {:answer/id #uuid "4321ec57-3aa3-5435-bef9-7196ceb02e4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{x}{x+1}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276073} {:answer/id #uuid "432e387d-bcda-51fe-936c-0efd0034da26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{1}{x+1}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{x}{(x+1)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276074} {:answer/id #uuid "50b08d4f-b9f3-5da2-9255-56d9e80228dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{x}{x+1}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{4}}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276197} {:answer/id #uuid "767527c1-5b06-5f0f-af49-a862e51ad660" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{x}{x+1}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276547}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "50b08d4f-b9f3-5da2-9255-56d9e80228dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{x}{x+1}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{4}}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276197} :answer-field/id #uuid "977aba5e-30b2-5f02-a704-7229f7441b6d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277994}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cbbd01ee-4c13-59a2-bf89-170f124c5003" :question/math-academy-id "q-161093" :question/problem "Use a fundamental vector product to determine which of the following integrals gives the surface area generated when the curve $y=\\frac{x}{x+1}$ is rotated by $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis for $x\\in [0,4].$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $y=\\frac{x}{x+1}\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in [0,4].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨x,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,(x,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)=\\frac{x}{x+1},$ and\n\n$$\nD={(x,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 4,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂x}⟩=⟨1,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨0,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f{f}^{′}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +f{f}^{′}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f{f}^{′},\\,-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖ & =\\sqrt{(f{f}^{′})^{2}+(-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}+{f}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}+{f}^{2}} \\\\  & =f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\cdot \\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)=\\frac{x}{x+1},$ we obtain ${f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}.$ So, finally, we conclude that our expression for the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{x}{x+1}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{x}{x+1}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{4}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278378 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1504b562-ea07-5020-baa3-bce4b8c73fa1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{5}\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{4}{{z}^{6}}}\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275675} {:answer/id #uuid "1f9511a3-47fe-51ed-b124-e1105e4f0c92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{5}\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{2}{{z}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275766} {:answer/id #uuid "8ba037df-7fdf-5532-a494-ac03592db52b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{5}\\frac{1}{{z}^{4}}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{2}{{z}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276747} {:answer/id #uuid "b5e00aa8-6165-5f31-8310-6e1e1a406117" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{5}\\frac{1}{{z}^{4}}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{4}{{z}^{6}}}\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277096} {:answer/id #uuid "f908b8f9-5e49-5941-bd3e-e05f0081a353" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{5}\\frac{1}{{z}^{4}}\\sqrt{1-\\frac{1}{{z}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277656}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1504b562-ea07-5020-baa3-bce4b8c73fa1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{5}\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{4}{{z}^{6}}}\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275675} :answer-field/id #uuid "11ef57f9-a018-5ee8-a2b1-622897978c22" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277759}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8a98dcae-fa38-562f-8676-270d1756205a" :question/math-academy-id "q-161099" :question/problem "Use a fundamental vector product to determine which of the following integrals gives the surface area generated when the curve $y=\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}$ is rotated by $2\\pi $ radians about the $z$-axis for $z\\in [1,5].$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $y=\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\ge 0$ for all $z\\in [1,5].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨f(z)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(z)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩,\\,(z,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(z)=\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}},$ and\n\n$$\nD={(z,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le z\\le 5,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{z}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂z},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂z},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂z}⟩=⟨{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}f{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{f}^{′}f{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}f⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖ & =\\sqrt{(-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+({f}^{′}f)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}+{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}} \\\\  & =f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{5}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{5}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\cdot \\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{5}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(z)=\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}},$ we obtain ${f}^{′}(z)=-\\frac{2}{{z}^{3}}.$ So, finally, we conclude that our expression for the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{5}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{5}\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\sqrt{1+{(-\\frac{2}{{z}^{3}})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{5}\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{4}{{z}^{6}}}\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278381 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2410c5d2-ad7f-56ab-b8c6-a7aad16f515d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.5\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275794} {:answer/id #uuid "3514b170-e1eb-53a5-9e81-17de317fd7f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.5\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275950} {:answer/id #uuid "5fb8bc3d-ff67-57f3-ae8b-552e2e72e1a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276333} {:answer/id #uuid "c02fcd5b-0741-5682-8bdc-226b818d2249" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277183} {:answer/id #uuid "e8422d6a-5c26-57c8-91a3-e854994c2b26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277509}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c02fcd5b-0741-5682-8bdc-226b818d2249" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277183} :answer-field/id #uuid "54bd7b28-8ea6-5b14-9542-32ed882c403b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277870}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1a46e83d-b704-567c-8d00-1886b9f6bc9b" :question/math-academy-id "q-161107" :question/problem "![](images/ef/ef8379b21526455a723369fda4360e2d7d3ef3cf54ce7d7767df7bd160a169f4.png)\n\nThe area of ​​the triangle $△ABC$ is $5\\sqrt{3}\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}.$ Find $AC.$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}bc\\operatorname{sin}⁡A,\n$$\n\nwhere $∠A$ is between the sides of lengths $b$ and $c.$ \n\nHere\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =AB=5\\text{ cm}, \\\\ b & =AC, \\\\ m∠A & ={60}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{60}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\n$$\n\nwe can calculate $b=AC,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot c\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ 5\\sqrt{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot 5\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{60}^{∘} \\\\ 5\\sqrt{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot 5\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\ 5\\sqrt{3} & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}\\,b}{4} \\\\ \\cancel{5\\sqrt{3}} & =\\frac{\\cancel{5\\sqrt{3}}\\,b}{4} \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{b}{4} \\\\ b & =4\\text{ cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that $AC=4\\,\\text{cm}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278383 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a92871f-5bd5-581e-ac06-dbd13021c053" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275724} {:answer/id #uuid "299f96e2-93fa-5d8f-ab41-fc313dbd3222" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.5\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275854} {:answer/id #uuid "4f831c2c-96bc-514e-94a8-9c7ae9a2e9a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276188} {:answer/id #uuid "7bb62d91-e7c1-5a0b-9594-0da656f39663" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.5\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276601} {:answer/id #uuid "a89eff61-0284-58bc-8453-86df1e739eb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276998}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a92871f-5bd5-581e-ac06-dbd13021c053" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275724} :answer-field/id #uuid "e821e218-a2a4-5efe-86d2-0f9a4556c28f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278132}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "43fd23a0-08b8-58ee-b7e6-43240cfa05d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-161109" :question/problem "![](images/b9/b95571772af958b4f7b7dcdbba630b78e029c838340dc272809a6c7915dc9c90.png)\n\nThe area of ​​the triangle $△ABC$ is $\\frac{21\\sqrt{2}}{2}{\\text{ cm}}^{2}.$ Find $AC.$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}bc\\operatorname{sin}⁡A,\n$$\n\nwhere $∠A$ is between the sides of lengths $b$ and $c.$ \n\nHere\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =AB=6\\text{ cm}, \\\\ b & =AC, \\\\ m∠A & ={45}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{45}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\n$$\n\nwe can calculate $b=AC,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot c\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ \\frac{21\\sqrt{2}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot 6\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{45}^{∘} \\\\ \\frac{21\\sqrt{2}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot 6\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{21\\sqrt{2}}{2} & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}\\,b}{2} \\\\ \\frac{21\\cancel{\\sqrt{2}}}{\\cancel{2}} & =\\frac{3\\cancel{\\sqrt{2}}\\,b}{\\cancel{2}} \\\\ 3b & =21 \\\\ b & =7\\text{ cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that $AC=7\\,\\text{cm}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278390 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45948700-503f-551b-bdea-87109566160f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+4{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276090} {:answer/id #uuid "4ab93a6e-5eac-5228-b299-b866a1ad303c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+2{x}^{2}+8{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276135} {:answer/id #uuid "6e807a78-b474-5a7f-8440-4f35e51cb60e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+8{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276468} {:answer/id #uuid "81b78249-6d6c-5a58-8ea3-ac7673969a7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{1+2{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276646} {:answer/id #uuid "ecdcb59c-b7f0-5394-8e00-a4e1644424c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277554}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ecdcb59c-b7f0-5394-8e00-a4e1644424c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277554} :answer-field/id #uuid "69336c1f-3dc5-5f03-92c4-84bacf0d2e8f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277910}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "128c01a3-ffea-59eb-9892-8068500c0400" :question/math-academy-id "q-161126" :question/problem "$$\n{∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A,\\,D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nThe integral above gives the surface area of $z={x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$ What is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz={x}^{2}+2{y}^{2},\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1.\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+2{y}^{2})=2x, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+2{y}^{2})=4y,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(2x)^{2}+(4y)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278392 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ad4c485-991a-5037-b69d-883685454737" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+36{x}^{4}{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275727} {:answer/id #uuid "870ff709-7130-5f4a-824d-3df0deccd82e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{1-3{x}^{2}+2y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276691} {:answer/id #uuid "b05684f5-deb4-54a0-b913-186656656d6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{1+9{x}^{4}+4{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277055} {:answer/id #uuid "b76e9e2d-b3d1-5d08-a8a9-414b1e3a6f7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-3{x}^{2}+2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277106} {:answer/id #uuid "e2342e6a-26fb-5edd-a09d-f48dbbcb61cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{1+36{x}^{4}{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277457}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b05684f5-deb4-54a0-b913-186656656d6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{1+9{x}^{4}+4{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277055} :answer-field/id #uuid "89ba9dfd-f03d-578b-831f-8a7946d17b7b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277970}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b62215b7-0e69-5d45-8968-8bb0a1bc12ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-161131" :question/problem "$$\n{∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A,\\,D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\nThe integral above gives the surface area of $z={y}^{2}-{x}^{3}$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$ What is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz={y}^{2}-{x}^{3},\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2.\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}-{x}^{3})=-3{x}^{2}, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}-{x}^{3})=2y,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(-3{x}^{2})}^{2}+{(2y)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+9{x}^{4}+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\sqrt{1+9{x}^{4}+4{y}^{2}}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278397 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e1d4d80-59ab-5ead-8c78-767fd4b1e908" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}\\sqrt{2+9{y}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275593} {:answer/id #uuid "35aef836-1879-55df-a3ee-bdb4fee77c7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}\\sqrt{1+y}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275956} {:answer/id #uuid "5552d5d8-21b2-5826-9b06-0d57a1ca45e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}2+3{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276235} {:answer/id #uuid "5a1b7425-0593-5bfc-8a0c-244f08247594" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}\\sqrt{2+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276285} {:answer/id #uuid "a43e0aa8-b0f3-53aa-a270-9f7badeb4b12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}\\sqrt{1+3{y}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276957}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e1d4d80-59ab-5ead-8c78-767fd4b1e908" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}\\sqrt{2+9{y}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275593} :answer-field/id #uuid "e75d780b-e75e-5b25-86f0-f1f9572d8822" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278128}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c11e996e-10b6-5010-bc4a-0d91257a3dfe" :question/math-academy-id "q-161139" :question/problem "Consider the portion of the surface $z=x+{y}^{3}$ whose projection onto the $xy$-plane is bounded by $y={x}^{2}$ and $y=1.$ Find a repeated integral expression for the surface area of this surface." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz=x+{y}^{3},\\,(x,y)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 1\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/87/87845c65dba220978ca81bd5a2b27816ca15f951cd14d8fe499ab43d3c64ef77.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+{y}^{3})=1, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+{y}^{3})=3{y}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(1)^{2}+{(3{y}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{2+9{y}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}\\sqrt{2+9{y}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278398 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "006bcbfc-d28a-5f99-aa49-be2cd9602e94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+y}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275483} {:answer/id #uuid "22090efc-b5f3-5f43-ac36-b7fe4c4588db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\sqrt{1+4{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275783} {:answer/id #uuid "545a0196-2f20-52f1-bf5a-4cf4121796a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\sqrt{1+{sin}^{2}⁡x+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276225} {:answer/id #uuid "5e3366ce-9fb2-57e8-b87c-6c8c24bfc9a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\sqrt{2+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276318} {:answer/id #uuid "7056a72c-612b-5f7f-841e-1d1c109d83f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\sqrt{2+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276489}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7056a72c-612b-5f7f-841e-1d1c109d83f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\sqrt{2+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276489} :answer-field/id #uuid "36e5eaa4-5870-549b-aad4-063ad6b17389" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277809}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "134aff90-1d55-5793-9da0-c59e5f46f5e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-161140" :question/problem "Consider the portion of the surface $z=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+y$ whose projection onto the $xy$-plane is bounded by the curve $y=\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}},$ and the $x$-axis. Find a repeated integral expression for the surface area of this surface." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+y,\\,(x,y)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/8b/8bf7a5921936b44a2e672fcee524c5a230800e6130f6c0a8683e6ce46829df58.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+y)=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{2}+(1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{2+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\sqrt{2+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278400 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f26c9a8-c236-5b49-a03a-a9c4e306b3f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276025} {:answer/id #uuid "56116490-cd88-5ee8-914f-70566119e239" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276242} {:answer/id #uuid "a4867883-7969-53b3-9ce6-9041f96c5468" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276959} {:answer/id #uuid "a7ede688-6fec-5d91-b089-01387832a30f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276993} {:answer/id #uuid "fcf48e5d-5a33-563d-899e-f6003b1ae552" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277694}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a4867883-7969-53b3-9ce6-9041f96c5468" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276959} :answer-field/id #uuid "85db4747-8718-5aa2-9149-1e2cb6cc044a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277963}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "433fa395-a287-58c1-aa07-6bcf4cb6b740" :question/math-academy-id "q-161144" :question/problem "Find the surface area of the portion of the surface $z=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\sqrt{2}y$ whose projection onto the $xy$-plane is bounded by $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$ and the $x$-axis from $x=0$ to $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\sqrt{2}y,\\,(x,y)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/f6/f6200343142274ee2f5b3934cbe014ee24edf8177f3a544c38dc070bb7df83ef.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\sqrt{2}y)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\sqrt{2}y)=2\\sqrt{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{2}+(2\\sqrt{2})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+9{cos}^{2}⁡x+8}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{9(1+{cos}^{2}⁡x)}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡x}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot y\\,{|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting $u=1+{cos}^{2}⁡x,$ we get $\\text{d}u=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x.$ Also, the limits must be changed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 0 & \\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ u & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting these into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =3{\\int }_{2}^{1}\\sqrt{u}(-\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2{u}^{3/2}\\,{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =2({2}^{3/2}-1) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278401 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19462bbc-e8e3-5c91-9788-201db0ff0e7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275712} {:answer/id #uuid "3e14d5ff-c5b0-51b3-a64c-0c5829abfe51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276016} {:answer/id #uuid "5230621b-8e33-5fe4-95e5-910062762b2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276209} {:answer/id #uuid "f8f7e2b2-6611-5828-aa36-89ee5ff2958f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277655} {:answer/id #uuid "fa2c4a43-6e97-511e-ae24-2d00cfcb639c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277666}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e14d5ff-c5b0-51b3-a64c-0c5829abfe51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276016} :answer-field/id #uuid "50b305b8-af28-529c-8dae-1d5802eb49f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277861}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2a3ad5a7-79b4-5ae2-b579-811466f8e968" :question/math-academy-id "q-161155" :question/problem "Find the surface area of the portion of the cone $z=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ that is bounded by the constraints $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $1\\le y\\le 2.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,1\\le y\\le 2.\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}})^{2}+(\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+1}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot (1-0)\\cdot (2-1) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278402 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02ddc779-96d9-5d16-aac4-0dabf1e17a89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275502} {:answer/id #uuid "30e154fd-4497-5263-9b08-b6765b29c0f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275911} {:answer/id #uuid "631f8d2b-2496-59aa-99b5-1012433451b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276375} {:answer/id #uuid "676c7ba3-971e-57ed-8e51-801a143984bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276403} {:answer/id #uuid "db0668ec-9a7d-5423-8d91-6e6280c3e7dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277397}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "631f8d2b-2496-59aa-99b5-1012433451b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276375} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a3e6e60-777f-5683-818b-00f5995e5b43" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277944}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bbda2ff7-05a0-5600-96fa-c9dfe7d1924f" :question/math-academy-id "q-161156" :question/problem "Find the surface area of the portion of the cylinder $z=\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}$ that is bounded by the constraints $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz=\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}},\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2.\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}})=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}})=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}})^{2}+(0)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\cdot y\\,{|}_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting $u=\\frac{x}{2},$ we get $\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}x.$ Also, the limits must be changed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 0 & 1 \\\\ u & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting these into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot 2\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u\\,{|}_{0}^{1/2} \\\\  & =4(\\frac{\\pi }{6}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278404 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1345328c-d6f4-52f6-a50a-dfaeca58fda8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\pi \\sqrt{6}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275654} {:answer/id #uuid "5bb82ad8-ecc6-50fb-a005-2657818a4003" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi \\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276299} {:answer/id #uuid "89c7d3f9-cb05-5b6e-8f6c-3becc6ff3485" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276722} {:answer/id #uuid "a4a9609e-3d6e-55a8-8aa4-446ec434eeb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\pi (\\sqrt{6}-1)}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276960} {:answer/id #uuid "c6e64f96-04c2-5753-bd3d-45634f0c1247" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi \\sqrt{6}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277245}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1345328c-d6f4-52f6-a50a-dfaeca58fda8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\pi \\sqrt{6}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275654} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b1140ff-6cec-5c9f-9667-884f9e1954ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277888}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bd23390c-f0cc-59ec-891d-3ea7fdf2c7b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-161164" :question/problem "Find the surface area of the part of the plane $z=2y-x$ that is subject to the constraints ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 9$ and $x\\le 0.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz=2y-x,\\,(x,y)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 9,\\,x\\le 0\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/3c/3c6c556ec90138fa1f0b599c6afed9d093e5c1b10f7751a5fbe55f0add617e1d.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y-x)=-1, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y-x)=2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(-1)^{2}+(2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+1+4}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region $D$ can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\sqrt{6}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{6}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{3}r\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}\\cdot [\\theta ]_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\cdot {[\\frac{{r}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}\\cdot \\pi \\cdot \\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi \\sqrt{6}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278405 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5900e7cc-ee45-5d65-83c7-79634d722251" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi (3\\sqrt{3}-1)}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276279} {:answer/id #uuid "771cd218-1dd3-5c3b-bd2a-7b6fa8dc4a0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi (7\\sqrt{7}-1)}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276554} {:answer/id #uuid "a397a0c2-4925-5f76-9203-58a1637fc98d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi (37\\sqrt{37}-1)}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276951} {:answer/id #uuid "e05145e3-0ebd-50ab-9f09-90871a53013b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi (\\sqrt{7}-1)}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277435} {:answer/id #uuid "e98fb1dd-4f16-5af1-b2eb-a146c009ae59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi (\\sqrt{37}-1)}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277519}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a397a0c2-4925-5f76-9203-58a1637fc98d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi (37\\sqrt{37}-1)}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276951} :answer-field/id #uuid "61d25ed5-dd77-5af5-aea4-178ccc88b158" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277899}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "95ffc39b-753c-5fd6-923e-0b13c7adc887" :question/math-academy-id "q-161165" :question/problem "Find the surface area of the part of the surface $z=9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ that is subject to the constraint ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 9.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz=9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2},\\,(x,y)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 9},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/d2/d2774aa4a85d25c4e35f3eb327adc82f9ef02eceb2be31ddf730493797e4cac2.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2x, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2y,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(-2x)^{2}+(-2y)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+4({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region $D$ can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+4({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{3}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting $u=1+4{r}^{2},$ we get $\\text{d}u=8r\\,\\text{d}r.$ Also, the limits must be changed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & 0 & 3 \\\\ u & 1 & 37\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting these into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r & =2\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{1}^{37}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}{\\int }_{1}^{37}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{[{u}^{3/2}]}_{1}^{37} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}{[{u}^{3/2}]}_{1}^{37} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}[(37)^{3/2}-1] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi (37\\sqrt{37}-1)}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278406 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "135454e4-1120-519b-8902-0052c3683dad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi (1-\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275655} {:answer/id #uuid "38f4f294-2474-5ce3-90de-838266743040" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi (2-\\sqrt{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275980} {:answer/id #uuid "4e6fca20-67c7-5d8e-8d89-12c47dd52ac9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}(1-\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276171} {:answer/id #uuid "acf33122-dba0-5c13-9a3a-5b9491194170" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi (2-\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277033} {:answer/id #uuid "c0d82ada-d288-5161-b0d8-ce0977b5f48e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi (3-\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277191}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "acf33122-dba0-5c13-9a3a-5b9491194170" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi (2-\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277033} :answer-field/id #uuid "7cc831c5-e9a8-5859-af7d-581c65abc8a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277946}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "66ec1dd0-f28a-513c-a9f4-29ac282d0de0" :question/math-academy-id "q-161167" :question/problem "Find the surface area of the part of the hemisphere $z=\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}$ that is subject to the constraints ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1$ and $y\\ge 0.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz=\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}},\\,(x,y)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1,\\,y\\ge 0\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/e7/e7cb4d16f39d1c1e38d65000471cbc5ca1831a0bbc2cfc61c4f03d087d6a5125.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}})}^{2}+{(-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region $D$ can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4-{r}^{2}}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2r}{\\sqrt{4-{r}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2r}{\\sqrt{4-{r}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2r}{\\sqrt{4-{r}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting $u=4-{r}^{2},$ we get $\\text{d}u=-2r\\,\\text{d}r.$ Also, the limits must be changed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & 0 & 1 \\\\ u & 4 & 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting these into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2r}{\\sqrt{4-{r}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}r & =-\\pi {\\int }_{4}^{3}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{3}^{4}{u}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\pi \\cdot 2{({u}^{1/2})}_{3}^{4} \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot ({4}^{1/2}-{3}^{1/2}) \\\\  & =2\\pi (2-\\sqrt{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278407 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fe3a77c-800e-5f02-af28-ff75516852cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi (37\\sqrt{37}-1)}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275615} {:answer/id #uuid "1a2092ad-7785-5787-b333-6d0fce8ca8f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi (\\sqrt{23}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275721} {:answer/id #uuid "6cc469de-7af1-5811-a2fa-a41c24f21c5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi (\\sqrt{37}-1)}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276454} {:answer/id #uuid "c7f27509-b929-5918-967e-009c7bf1b045" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi (3\\sqrt{15}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277253} {:answer/id #uuid "ebd6bc52-08f6-52ed-bfc8-71ce0195e169" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi (17\\sqrt{15}-1)}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277543}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0fe3a77c-800e-5f02-af28-ff75516852cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi (37\\sqrt{37}-1)}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275615} :answer-field/id #uuid "944c8782-8047-5b05-afcc-d1e7c99a7d62" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277986}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b5e36073-07ed-5be9-b9f9-14a5dbeadf43" :question/math-academy-id "q-161173" :question/problem "Find the surface area of the part of the hyperbolic paraboloid $x={z}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ that is subject to the constraints ${y}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 9$ and $z\\le 0.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $x=f(y,z)$ with $(y,z)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂y})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nx={z}^{2}-{y}^{2},\\,(y,z)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD=\\{(y,z)\\,:\\,{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 9,\\,z\\le 0\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/cd/cd5bcd7857769afb5f3a4f6197757c08eb295c7075a271c302c4b6087d661d14.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({z}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2y, \\\\ \\frac{∂x}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({z}^{2}-{y}^{2})=2z,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂y})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(-2y)^{2}+(2z)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+4{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+4({y}^{2}+{z}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region $D$ can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+4({y}^{2}+{z}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{3}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting $u=1+4{r}^{2},$ we get $\\text{d}u=8r\\,\\text{d}r.$ Also, the limits must be changed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & 0 & 3 \\\\ u & 1 & 37\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting these into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r & =\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{1}^{37}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}{\\int }_{1}^{37}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{[{u}^{3/2}]}_{1}^{37} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{12}{[{u}^{3/2}]}_{1}^{37} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{12}[(37)^{3/2}-1] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi (37\\sqrt{37}-1)}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278408 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e2fe78b-4cd0-558d-9e93-31f26208dc71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{3}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276017} {:answer/id #uuid "3e7778ed-117c-5fa1-acc1-b2c97b01b3ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi (\\sqrt{3}+1)}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276018} {:answer/id #uuid "56775f02-8b2b-5019-9e46-91e136692e31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276248} {:answer/id #uuid "9d6135d4-8f96-520b-8b96-abd5e27a8317" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276890} {:answer/id #uuid "aca71121-a3e0-5db8-81bc-cf1fa06db8de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277029}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e2fe78b-4cd0-558d-9e93-31f26208dc71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{3}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276017} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b61f550-2b34-5e06-841f-8c7efed4d644" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277775}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7d00a6aa-63ec-5be5-aa3c-4e85a0b4a1af" :question/math-academy-id "q-161174" :question/problem "Find the surface area of the part of the plane $x=2-y-z$ that is subject to the constraints ${y}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 1$ and $y\\ge 0,\\,z\\ge 0.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $x=f(y,z)$ with $(y,z)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂y})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nx=2-y-z,\\,(y,z)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD=\\{(y,z)\\,:\\,{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 1,\\,y\\ge 0,\\,z\\ge 0\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/37/3741c5db05232f04f60f747dc04176bfc77da30bbc39c79888cf4c18939369f7.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2-y-z)=-1, \\\\ \\frac{∂x}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2-y-z)=-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂y})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+1+1}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region $D$ can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\sqrt{3}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{1}r\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot [\\theta ]_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\cdot {[\\frac{{r}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{3}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278409 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b69cd4c-0c3e-5f84-a667-3e998671dc1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{13}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275871} {:answer/id #uuid "78922260-8fb0-5574-b9fe-40db1c7a986b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276570} {:answer/id #uuid "7d29f2a0-987c-5e06-bd1c-9d69b2646c79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13\\pi \\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276618} {:answer/id #uuid "9909b8d8-f47f-5d7d-ae9b-ab6300fee337" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276851} {:answer/id #uuid "fff26cfa-64e7-5943-9ce1-56d10da11f45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13\\pi \\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277726}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9909b8d8-f47f-5d7d-ae9b-ab6300fee337" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276851} :answer-field/id #uuid "e948b81b-2e80-5712-b122-75bcc52059e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278137}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fedbba32-290f-52d2-807e-9187e81e100a" :question/math-academy-id "q-161176" :question/problem "Find the surface area of the part of the hyperbolic paraboloid $y=1-{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ that is subject to the constraint ${x}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 2.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $y=f(x,z)$ with $(x,z)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\ny=1-{x}^{2}+{z}^{2},\\,(x,z)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 2\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/ab/ab855ef2b9b5363005c14ea37470ed9ecb5853380a301341d20e2cd6095a5d1f.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂y}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1-{x}^{2}+{z}^{2})=-2x, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(1-{x}^{2}+{z}^{2})=2z,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(-2x)^{2}+(2z)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+4({x}^{2}+{z}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region $D$ can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le \\sqrt{2},\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+4({x}^{2}+{z}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting $u=1+4{r}^{2},$ we get $\\text{d}u=8r\\,\\text{d}r.$ Also, the limits must be changed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & 0 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ u & 1 & 9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting these into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r & =2\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{1}^{9}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}{\\int }_{1}^{9}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{[{u}^{3/2}]}_{1}^{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}{[{u}^{3/2}]}_{1}^{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}(27-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{13\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278410 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3427080d-6e07-595f-8bdf-12b82df47007" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(5\\sqrt{5}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275939} {:answer/id #uuid "42b74163-07b3-59fe-a577-429799e99107" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{9}(\\sqrt{5}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276064} {:answer/id #uuid "658efc61-0551-5e6f-a3d1-14f7c8f8d9a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}(\\sqrt{10}-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276389} {:answer/id #uuid "d57fe3a4-c6d8-5413-a5a4-03161fc89524" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10\\sqrt{10}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277356} {:answer/id #uuid "f1c185fa-40eb-5867-a9e7-4b8abc10c7fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8\\sqrt{2}}{9}(5\\sqrt{5}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277600}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1c185fa-40eb-5867-a9e7-4b8abc10c7fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8\\sqrt{2}}{9}(5\\sqrt{5}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277600} :answer-field/id #uuid "96482818-83a7-5e7b-bd3f-117b12c337e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277992}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3dce2ffe-4326-5f45-948b-458dac5baa92" :question/math-academy-id "q-161177" :question/problem "Find the surface area of the part of the surface $y=\\frac{4}{7}{x}^{7/4}+z$ whose projection onto the $xz$-plane is bounded by $z=\\sqrt{x},$ $x=4,$ and the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $y=f(x,z)$ with $(x,z)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{4}{7}{x}^{7/4}+z,\\,(x,z)\\in D,\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 4,\\,0\\le z\\le \\sqrt{x}\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/0a/0a4ed826b683e7e8d8ed3e05bcb75618e74314febcdda8ae31b09f379ca21c73.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂y}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{4}{7}{x}^{7/4}+z)={x}^{3/4}, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\frac{4}{7}{x}^{7/4}+z)=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{({x}^{3/4})}^{2}+(1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{2+{x}^{3/2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{x}}\\sqrt{2+{x}^{3/2}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}\\sqrt{2+{x}^{3/2}}\\cdot z\\,{|}_{z=0}^{z=\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}\\sqrt{2+{x}^{3/2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting $u=2+{x}^{3/2},$ we get $\\text{d}u=\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Also, the limits must be changed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 0 & 4 \\\\ u & 2 & 10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting these into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\sqrt{2+{x}^{3/2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{2}^{10}\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{9}{u}^{3/2}\\,{|}_{2}^{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{9}({10}^{3/2}-{2}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{9}(10\\sqrt{10}-2\\sqrt{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\sqrt{2}}{9}(5\\sqrt{5}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278413 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "197b15d9-86e4-5810-b41c-df9b8c94d9e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275716} {:answer/id #uuid "58893840-44db-5075-a0b5-b32a15b2f771" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276274} {:answer/id #uuid "77c1e4ce-be45-56d8-bbb7-1b1ab3afb3cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276561} {:answer/id #uuid "b57666b6-8a5d-5dc6-82d6-fb7e4a709773" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277095} {:answer/id #uuid "e78d96df-d25c-5987-8053-3666f6f8f153" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277502}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "197b15d9-86e4-5810-b41c-df9b8c94d9e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275716} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f67d574-ed79-5483-9cb1-dcd07a759e5b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277924}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a172a4da-f55b-58e5-9a2f-32af9ff9ae04" :question/math-academy-id "q-161184" :question/problem "Find the surface area of the portion of the surface $z=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}$ whose projection onto the $xy$-plane is bounded by $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}$ and the $x$-axis from $x=-1$ to $x=1.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2},\\,(x,y)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/b9/b9504140a4827cd74b74e8c23f76e1305389f1bc93a87834a37d4b20222e56f0.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})=x, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(x)^{2}+(0)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1/\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}\\cdot y\\,{|}_{0}^{1/\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\,{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =1-(-1) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278423 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2267c827-eb1c-5a49-8945-8b57c2705f76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275785} {:answer/id #uuid "2acea8cc-65a6-557e-aebb-f00b7a1d47e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275869} {:answer/id #uuid "2ee307ba-0917-5eaf-9a2d-cfd683dd6dda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275897} {:answer/id #uuid "9f8ec9a1-767b-56b7-9a74-ea4c161e6168" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276913} {:answer/id #uuid "ed373588-11a1-511a-b75c-000a157f60e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277557}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ed373588-11a1-511a-b75c-000a157f60e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277557} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5b44e56-8986-5454-81e7-50391bee6973" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278046}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cf3b06c6-a6ea-5b36-a93d-dea2e36cbc84" :question/math-academy-id "q-161205" :question/problem "![](images/65/658f58654e8791f15fe6c2a9c719de3b02aa1c3912b4b6dc7b4e2fe2d2cdda62.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,(3-z)\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of a right circular cylinder of height $3$ whose base is a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $z$-component of $F=⟨0,\\,0,\\,(3-z)\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral side of $S$ is parallel to the $z$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the top and bottom sides. \n\n![](images/05/05664db64ac6595ff0caec7daeec20cb3508d5c18a5266aa126295e3aa040233.png)\n\n \nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the top surface. On this surface, we have $z=3$ for ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1.$ Therefore, for everywhere on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nF(x,y,0)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,(3-3)\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}⟩=⟨0,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}0\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}0\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the bottom surface. On this surface, we have $z=0$ for ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{2}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=0$ on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,(3-z)\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,(3-0)\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =-3\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}-3\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =-3{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying a change of variables $(x,y)\\to (r,\\theta ),\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & =-3{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-3{∬}_{D}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =-3\\cdot 2\\pi \\cdot {[\\frac{1}{3}{r}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-6\\pi \\cdot (\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =-2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =0+(-2\\pi ) \\\\  & =-2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278428 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "320a1755-e69e-5cd0-a46c-819502a4a8d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}(2\\sqrt{2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275919} {:answer/id #uuid "6e4265cf-1e9f-5176-b7e0-53f3bd0567fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276465} {:answer/id #uuid "a548d80b-dea9-5a3b-80fe-5ff52033d595" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}-1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276968} {:answer/id #uuid "cde487b7-7b95-53ad-9673-7186a03f8321" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277301} {:answer/id #uuid "ecfdf384-cd12-5988-9068-e2f63ac7af66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277555}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "320a1755-e69e-5cd0-a46c-819502a4a8d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}(2\\sqrt{2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275919} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a7abe6b-a9b9-5557-8bb8-9589e2527386" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278000}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1d826d14-2298-585c-90fc-f27a43f9e900" :question/math-academy-id "q-161213" :question/problem "Find the surface area of the part of the surface $x={y}^{2}+\\sqrt{3}z$ whose projection onto the $yz$-plane is bounded by $z=y,$ $y=1,$ and the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $x=f(y,z)$ with $(y,z)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂y})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nx={y}^{2}+\\sqrt{3}z,\\,(y,z)\\in D,\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,0\\le z\\le y},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/0c/0cf571155f2a4cd78d83c4808326cd8102badb3c22f43e00d95aee2494859f48.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}+\\sqrt{3}z)=2y, \\\\ \\frac{∂x}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({y}^{2}+\\sqrt{3}z)=\\sqrt{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂y})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(2y)^{2}+(\\sqrt{3})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{4+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2\\sqrt{1+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{y}\\sqrt{1+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{1+{y}^{2}}\\cdot z\\,{|}_{z=0}^{z=y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{1+{y}^{2}}\\cdot y\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting $u=1+{y}^{2},$ we get $\\text{d}u=2y\\,\\text{d}y.$ Also, the limits must be changed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & 0 & 1 \\\\ u & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting these into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{1+{y}^{2}}\\cdot y\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}\\,{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}({2}^{3/2}-{1}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(2\\sqrt{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278429 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fc235a2-69a7-56fd-ab60-88369ca3597f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3(e+2)^{3/2}+9\\sqrt{3}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275769} {:answer/id #uuid "6ecb222f-5eff-5885-bd05-541e193d3731" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-3\\sqrt{3}+e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276470} {:answer/id #uuid "7a209b18-88fa-51c4-94f1-1a2189c18e1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2(e-1)^{3/2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276586} {:answer/id #uuid "99ae597c-3a2e-53b6-a512-8da9aede5125" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{e+2}-\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276856} {:answer/id #uuid "cd431321-0991-589f-8644-2625a8488766" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(e+2)^{3/2}-3\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277300}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd431321-0991-589f-8644-2625a8488766" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(e+2)^{3/2}-3\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277300} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca8c0ead-ac6b-5ddf-9490-787f763c2907" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278082}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c338dfd1-e7f8-59f7-ac67-821e4b45c353" :question/math-academy-id "q-161214" :question/problem "Find the surface area of the part of the surface $x={e}^{y}-z$ whose projection onto the $yz$-plane is bounded by $z={e}^{2y},$ $y=0,$ $y=\\frac{1}{2},$ and the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $x=f(y,z)$ with $(y,z)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂y})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nx={e}^{y}-z,\\,(y,z)\\in D,\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nD=\\{(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{1}{2},\\,0\\le z\\le {e}^{2y}\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/9c/9cae5b68f77c2d79df8a81d0421146ac2bbe618c6065a457c33c86caceeb1327.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{y}-z)={e}^{y}, \\\\ \\frac{∂x}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({e}^{y}-z)=-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂y})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+({e}^{y})^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{e}^{2y}+2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}{\\int }_{0}^{{e}^{2y}}\\sqrt{{e}^{2y}+2}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\sqrt{{e}^{2y}+2}\\cdot z\\,{|}_{z=0}^{z={e}^{2y}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\sqrt{{e}^{2y}+2}\\cdot {e}^{2y}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting $u={e}^{2y}+2,$ we get $\\text{d}u=2{e}^{2y}\\,\\text{d}y.$ Also, the limits must be changed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & 0 & 1/2 \\\\ u & 3 & e+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting these into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\sqrt{{e}^{2y}+2}\\cdot {e}^{2y}\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{3}^{e+2}\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{3/2}\\,{|}_{3}^{e+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}((e+2)^{3/2}-{3}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(e+2)^{3/2}-3\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278431 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e9c78dc-55fa-58d4-a093-f9544e5dfbd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{28}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275601} {:answer/id #uuid "78632b46-36da-5697-b93d-bc2adc1c9045" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276568} {:answer/id #uuid "79740243-a948-508f-ad95-f7961fb345fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{19}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276581} {:answer/id #uuid "7c58ab40-2a38-5ea8-a5ef-7548298d0ad5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276612} {:answer/id #uuid "7c6fd983-c520-5566-9c80-71e44ea0a60f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276613}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c58ab40-2a38-5ea8-a5ef-7548298d0ad5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276612} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e7ff9e2-fb54-58a9-9896-204ca397e982" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277922}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "df8892a4-0ff3-5c81-818d-9a1c55b02265" :question/math-academy-id "q-161219" :question/problem "![](images/9a/9afc7f79f4612ce579e82a6b3c5086922d14051978907bfeb5c133a3f99791c7.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,x{y}^{2}+z⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of the rectangular solid shown above in the first octant. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $z$-component of $F=⟨0,\\,0,\\,x{y}^{2}+z⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral sides of $S$ are parallel to the $z$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the top and bottom sides. \n\n![](images/fa/fa4986b1de44fa4ccc75114e64abfd93d0de16618f2f1b29695d9115019b6e3e.png)\n\n \n\nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the top surface. On this surface, we have $z=4$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=4$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,x{y}^{2}+z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,x{y}^{2}+4⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =x{y}^{2}+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}x{y}^{2}+4\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x{y}^{2}+4\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}x{y}^{2}+4\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+4x]}_{x=0}^{x=2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2{y}^{2}+8\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3}+8y]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the bottom surface. On this surface, we have $z=0$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{2}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=0$ on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{2} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,x{y}^{2}+z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,x{y}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =-x{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}-x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y{\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{[\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}]}_{0}^{1}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{3}+(-\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278432 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71b4b278-b65f-5406-9647-519faf867137" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276501} {:answer/id #uuid "7305c0b2-d8c2-5e47-bbb3-3425beae0e64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276519} {:answer/id #uuid "9eb283a3-ac7c-5ffa-9921-e523b36374a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276903} {:answer/id #uuid "bca4ccd7-be58-541f-ae27-b6666d1303c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277157} {:answer/id #uuid "be2e5824-d10f-5cc6-81d8-eaf9f7d77d64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277175}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71b4b278-b65f-5406-9647-519faf867137" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276501} :answer-field/id #uuid "d99b0f37-11a6-51ae-b457-b0354976d304" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278108}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8c3f4c19-765d-5833-904a-bf7538da3049" :question/math-academy-id "q-161222" :question/problem "![](images/01/01902836fd1e91ac829f67a279103a9b3d5732d7b058f03c695b6733a42e673d.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}+z⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of the rectangular solid shown above in the first octant. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $z$-component of $F=⟨0,\\,0,\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}+z⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral sides of $S$ are parallel to the $z$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the top and bottom sides. \n\n![](images/c0/c058465d6ca7855446711ebc44f6b23913ee786d2fd9bb32ae6c671964e1ad32.png)\n\n \n\nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the top surface. On this surface, we have $z=1$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=1$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}+z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}+1⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & ={x}^{3}+{y}^{2}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}+1\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}+1\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}+1\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}+x{y}^{2}+x]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}+\\frac{5}{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}+\\frac{5}{4}y]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the bottom surface. On this surface, we have $z=0$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{2}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=0$ on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{2} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}+z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =-({x}^{3}+{y}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}-({x}^{3}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}+x{y}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{[\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}+\\frac{1}{4}y]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{12}+(-\\frac{7}{12}) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278435 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05ccebf2-82ed-5d83-b410-939ba5e5d92a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{1+9{y}^{4}+{e}^{2z}}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{9}\\le 1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275529} {:answer/id #uuid "2cc8786b-2181-586e-88b7-5dec8ae2aecc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{{y}^{3}+{e}^{2z}-1}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{9}\\ge 1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275880} {:answer/id #uuid "549bec80-04d3-5b1a-a54f-70468df126a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{2{y}^{4}+{e}^{z}}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{9}=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276229} {:answer/id #uuid "60d578d1-2b32-5020-837e-daeac36cf440" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{1+9{y}^{2}+{e}^{2z}}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{9}\\ge 1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276348} {:answer/id #uuid "c5030f28-22fb-5802-acf2-25497ced4080" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{2{y}^{2}+{e}^{z}}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{9}\\le 1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277230}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05ccebf2-82ed-5d83-b410-939ba5e5d92a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{1+9{y}^{4}+{e}^{2z}}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{9}\\le 1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275529} :answer-field/id #uuid "97154eb2-395c-5491-bba8-688c25fe0e0c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277993}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1e867d73-e633-518f-b45d-26ad639d5221" :question/math-academy-id "q-161230" :question/problem "$$\n{∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A,\\,D=\\{(y,z)\\,:\\,\\boxed{}\\,\\}\n$$\n\nThe integral above gives the surface area of the part of the surface $x={y}^{3}+{e}^{z}$ that lies inside the elliptical cylinder $\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{9}=1.$ From left to right, what are the missing expressions?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $x=f(y,z)$ with $(y,z)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂y})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nx={y}^{3}+{e}^{z},\\,\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{9}\\le 1.\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{3}+{e}^{z})=3{y}^{2}, \\\\ \\frac{∂x}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({y}^{3}+{e}^{z})={e}^{z},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂y})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(3{y}^{2})}^{2}+({e}^{z})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+9{y}^{4}+{e}^{2z}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is\n\n$\\,$ $\\sqrt{1+9{y}^{4}+{e}^{2z}}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{9}\\le 1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278453 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "013ad255-d6b2-53af-b247-bda74ed931eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275486} {:answer/id #uuid "242f490d-26c9-53d4-8858-aee448c3639a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275798} {:answer/id #uuid "57565970-bba8-51b5-a49d-102f87d70315" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276257} {:answer/id #uuid "7a528d6c-1f90-5b6a-8f52-05a122aa2a68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276588} {:answer/id #uuid "d7e711ec-d244-5ffb-ba86-154bff5c4604" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277377}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d7e711ec-d244-5ffb-ba86-154bff5c4604" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277377} :answer-field/id #uuid "f654561c-bcac-563f-a9e7-f8e969bf70dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278157}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "44ff0211-30a5-53e4-9da3-c64535815d63" :question/math-academy-id "q-161270" :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(x{y}^{2}{z}^{3}-z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the sum rule and write our repeated integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}x{y}^{2}{z}^{3}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}z\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nSince the domain of integration $R=[0,4]\\times [1,2]\\times [0,1]$ is a rectangular box, and each integrand can be expressed as $f(x)\\cdot g(y)\\cdot h(z),$ we can rewrite our sum of repeated integrals as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{4}x\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{1}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}{z}^{3}\\,\\text{d}z-{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\text{d}x{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\text{d}y\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}z\\,\\text{d}z\n$$\n\nLet's evaluate each of these separately:\n\n- For the first product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}x\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{1}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}{z}^{3}\\,\\text{d}z & ={[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{4}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}]}_{1}^{2}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{4}{z}^{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(8-0)(\\frac{8}{3}-\\frac{1}{3})(\\frac{1}{4}-0) \\\\  & =8(\\frac{7}{3})(\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\text{d}x{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\text{d}y\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}z\\,\\text{d}z & =(4-0)\\cdot (2-1)\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{z}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(x{y}^{2}{z}^{3}-z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{14}{3}-2=\\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278461 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0294c002-bd57-57d3-80b3-7fabcd4c0abb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275500} {:answer/id #uuid "0a1b7943-d74f-593a-a73d-4f7b2984e272" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275563} {:answer/id #uuid "1db67973-261d-5c72-9a22-797cdd70f57d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275749} {:answer/id #uuid "6f3d9f2b-1400-51de-a847-0f9dbed86615" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276476} {:answer/id #uuid "8a4f51d4-c721-5278-ac9c-1a3d3fac3e2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276726}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1db67973-261d-5c72-9a22-797cdd70f57d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275749} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a7c1739-5bdb-5824-990f-022e95db8fc5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277772}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d4391484-a70c-5d5d-8147-34baf59f21fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-161283" :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨z,\\,2,\\,-3xy⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by the equations\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u{v}^{2},\\,-1,\\,1-2u⟩\n$$\n\nfor $0\\le u\\le 1$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u{v}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-1),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(1-2u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{v}^{2},\\,0,\\,-2⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u{v}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-1),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(1-2u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2uv,\\,0,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ {v}^{2} & 0 & -2 \\\\ 2uv & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|,-|\\begin{aligned}{v}^{2} & -2 \\\\ 2uv & 0\\end{aligned}|,|\\begin{aligned}{v}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 2uv & 0\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,-4uv,\\,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & ={⟨z,\\,2,\\,-3xy⟩|}_{x=u{v}^{2}\\,y=-1\\,z=1-2u} \\\\  & =⟨1-2u,\\,2,\\,-3(u{v}^{2})(-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1-2u,\\,2,\\,3u{v}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨1-2u,\\,2,\\,3u{v}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,-4uv,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =(1-2u)\\cdot 0+2\\cdot (-4uv)+3u{v}^{2}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-8uv\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral evaluates to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨z,\\,2,\\,-3xy⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-8uv)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-8{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}uv\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-8{\\int }_{0}^{1}u\\,\\text{d}u{\\int }_{0}^{1}v\\,\\text{d}v\\, \\\\  & =-8\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}]}_{0}^{1}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-8(\\frac{1}{2})(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278465 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14cad042-e3b8-5970-b4bc-1bab2bf4016f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275672} {:answer/id #uuid "3b878478-8e1e-520b-a4db-1818b785afe6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275996} {:answer/id #uuid "4d0d09d4-8a11-5a5b-bec2-8c816863a4b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276159} {:answer/id #uuid "79cda5fc-2517-525f-8688-5a79879dccd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276583} {:answer/id #uuid "d1314b9f-3f1e-5892-ac6b-e18c0c36419f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277327}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b878478-8e1e-520b-a4db-1818b785afe6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275996} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf3fea7f-c3ee-5a03-bdbe-79ed9c6a39ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278088}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5610a24a-7851-5e97-8ed8-8bbb2532b7e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-161291" :question/problem "![](images/97/9795c04bf6d4bfa407a7b9176cd5c4b97b3d89bfcc6fff17d1d78190047b3a54.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}6y\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=x+y$ and whose domain is the upper half-circle of radius $1$ centered at $O,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+y)=1,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+y)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}6y\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}6y\\cdot \\sqrt{1+{1}^{2}+(1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}6y\\cdot \\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{D}\\,6y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{D}\\,6y\\,\\text{d}A & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{Δ}6r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\cdot \\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {[\\frac{{r}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}(1+1) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278468 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f9a66d7-87b7-58f9-a076-b54b892923b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275768} {:answer/id #uuid "7c15054a-734a-5d80-aa3f-8135e29198e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276609} {:answer/id #uuid "a3c9e5ba-3a49-5395-9f31-5cbdddda130a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276955} {:answer/id #uuid "ccb0ab77-c652-580f-b8b9-74b7d90211f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277296} {:answer/id #uuid "e186f105-1b84-51e2-8b84-167c98d9a5ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277449}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3c9e5ba-3a49-5395-9f31-5cbdddda130a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276955} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e6f0392-f1ae-5916-94a3-2c01a4575273" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277852}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "86501c09-fb5b-5eb4-8dc1-b7ee80036bf5" :question/math-academy-id "q-161300" :question/problem "![](images/01/0148ca16ee12a0f651c91091715b4ec07d95639530defb8ed366bacb33e4e87d.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}8xy\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=y-2x$ and whose domain is the quarter-circle of radius $2$ centered at $O$ in the first quadrant, as shown above." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y-2x)=-2,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y-2x)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}8xy\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}8xy\\cdot \\sqrt{1+(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}8xy\\cdot \\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{∬}_{D}\\,8xy\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{6}{∬}_{D}\\,8xy\\,\\text{d}A & =\\sqrt{6}{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{∬}_{Δ}8r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{∬}_{Δ}4{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{2}4{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta [{r}^{4}]_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}16\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{6}[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta ]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{6}(1+1) \\\\  & =16\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278469 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01ef1d15-ab53-5c1e-ae63-22e77b303a32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275493} {:answer/id #uuid "6732bd4b-5bae-5817-8033-888f275b28fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "56" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276400} {:answer/id #uuid "67a2f8f7-b5b8-58b8-9b71-9fb46c231333" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276404} {:answer/id #uuid "770b7a40-3fb3-5938-a6fd-72477b0e9ede" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276552} {:answer/id #uuid "af7eefd6-d587-5e8f-ac1d-55687b70130a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277049}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af7eefd6-d587-5e8f-ac1d-55687b70130a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277049} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c6d5d4c-02e0-5686-9189-c711d926bc87" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277847}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7e7a3fcc-a15c-5e4a-92b5-d91d52ab682a" :question/math-academy-id "q-161301" :question/problem "![](images/52/5276afad13c3e52854d33a3ff3404ab401781e08d1888214121cf2d56c0bb304.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}5x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=2x+2y$ and whose domain is the right half-circle of radius $2$ centered at $O$, as shown above." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x+2y)=2,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x+2y)=2.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}5x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}5x{y}^{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}5x{y}^{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{9}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =3{∬}_{D}\\,5x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{∬}_{D}\\,5x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & =3{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =3{∬}_{Δ}5r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot {r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{∬}_{Δ}5{r}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{2}5{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta [{r}^{5}]_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =96{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =96{[\\frac{{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta }{3}]}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =32(1+1) \\\\  & =64.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278475 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05c6bd5e-0a05-54b2-8760-1af815a21d72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275528} {:answer/id #uuid "67245e6c-b985-5f9e-a895-775b667aacaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276399} {:answer/id #uuid "77e3d978-da8c-5d68-b60a-4a8194925e3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276562} {:answer/id #uuid "78ac9cfc-a243-510f-9152-b7aa90fe5547" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276571} {:answer/id #uuid "d19e77ba-76c5-54e5-8b35-467bdaf0f222" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277329}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05c6bd5e-0a05-54b2-8760-1af815a21d72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275528} :answer-field/id #uuid "455d1d44-da82-5aaf-86ed-4b3d398efe8a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277834}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cc8526c3-3b66-50ba-a72f-6803fda09a5e" :question/math-academy-id "q-161311" :question/problem "![](images/5b/5ba2dbc746b9fe8b36202d31bbeb8193ffb9e747d6ed4c2e2f0f994499f88e8e.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=-x+\\sqrt{2}y$ and whose domain is the quarter-circle of radius $3$ centered at $O$ in the second quadrant, as shown above." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-x+\\sqrt{2}y)=-1,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-x+\\sqrt{2}y)=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+(-1)^{2}+(\\sqrt{2})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{4}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2{∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & =2{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =2{∬}_{Δ}\\frac{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot r\\text{ }\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{∬}_{Δ}\\frac{{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{∬}_{Δ}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{3}{[\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\,]}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{3}(-1)\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278477 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27d4b598-7e8d-541e-bbd6-3dbbb07b4ca3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275841} {:answer/id #uuid "4304b64b-94d9-5a07-9565-9ca1566abcf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276068} {:answer/id #uuid "4cf0425b-4482-59ac-acf5-655dac3d6002" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276156} {:answer/id #uuid "7347d307-a529-5778-9cc3-b43ce3b89820" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276522} {:answer/id #uuid "ec21c7c2-b53f-5a6f-9b9f-2e82eb49b46c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277547}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4304b64b-94d9-5a07-9565-9ca1566abcf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276068} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5d3d767-8116-56a7-b22f-ca77b6227aaf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278047}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "23220eb4-f0ea-5f75-a067-2a7409386baf" :question/math-academy-id "q-161318" :question/problem "![](images/5d/5d6cedcc2d8c5a232d8d7ed89f050fa7a545ed0da6075ddb44eec67e065b9e87.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}3{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=-4x+\\sqrt{8}y$ and whose domain is the upper half-circle of radius $1$ centered at $O$, as shown above." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-4x+\\sqrt{8}y)=-4,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-4x+\\sqrt{8}y)=\\sqrt{8}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}3{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3{x}^{2}y\\cdot \\sqrt{1+(-4)^{2}+(\\sqrt{8})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3{x}^{2}y\\cdot \\sqrt{25}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =5{∬}_{D}\\,3{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{∬}_{D}\\,3{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A & =5{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =5{∬}_{Δ}3{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =15{∬}_{Δ}{r}^{4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =15{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =15{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{[\\frac{{r}^{5}}{5}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={[-{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta ]}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278478 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a533bb1-853f-5491-8c86-67f976471508" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}\\,xy(y-2x)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275862} {:answer/id #uuid "51369986-90ae-5b8b-bd51-00e4a125fb18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}\\,x{y}^{2}(y+2x)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276202} {:answer/id #uuid "62b5397b-e1ef-56af-b3c3-0127ce34e303" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{x}^{2}y(y-2x)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276371} {:answer/id #uuid "7ea26c5b-44a3-5c69-9b86-1ab9c4524f0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}\\,{x}^{2}y(y-2x)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276627} {:answer/id #uuid "e533f6b6-2b56-5f69-89d2-aa4aa20f904b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}\\,xy(y-2x)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277478}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a533bb1-853f-5491-8c86-67f976471508" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}\\,xy(y-2x)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275862} :answer-field/id #uuid "56bb3622-f59e-504a-a378-a8b68a6075e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277880}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e62de12f-3877-52f7-8f85-f37913bbcbfa" :question/math-academy-id "q-161319" :question/problem "Consider the surface integral of the function $f(x,y,z)=xy{z}^{2},$ where the surface $S$ is defined by $z=y-2x$ for $-1\\le x\\le 1$ and $-1\\le y\\le 1.$\nThis surface integral can be written as a repeated integral, as shown below. \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y-2x)=-2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y-2x)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(-2)}^{2}+{(1)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\sqrt{6}\\,xy(y-2x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,\\sqrt{6}\\,xy(y-2x)^{2}\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278479 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "086549fa-2a26-5001-86f1-5b98b5f931b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}({x}^{2}y+{x}^{2}{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275551} {:answer/id #uuid "50ddfd56-d4fe-58e4-9068-9d6c2ddb54e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5x}(2{x}^{2}-x{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276200} {:answer/id #uuid "9dada5e0-74dc-5e81-a533-742d9452a01c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{5}({x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276893} {:answer/id #uuid "a82df97c-47b4-5649-adc4-f83468150b33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{2}({x}^{2}{y}^{2}+x{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276996} {:answer/id #uuid "bca9a670-f13c-561a-a08a-381f1f64ba75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{2}({x}^{2}y-x{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277158}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9dada5e0-74dc-5e81-a533-742d9452a01c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{5}({x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276893} :answer-field/id #uuid "055d6d61-c143-5db4-98a6-8b0a8ee7ad03" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277736}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "71274639-1883-57e5-9cbd-1e009801625d" :question/math-academy-id "q-161321" :question/problem "Consider the surface integral of the function $f(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{2}xyz,$ where the surface $S$ is defined by $z=-\\sqrt{2}x-\\sqrt{2}y$ for $-1\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$\nThis surface integral can be written as a repeated integral, as shown below. \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-\\sqrt{2}x-\\sqrt{2}y)=-\\sqrt{2},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-\\sqrt{2}x-\\sqrt{2}y)=-\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(-\\sqrt{2})}^{2}+{(-\\sqrt{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\sqrt{2}xy(-\\sqrt{2}x-\\sqrt{2}y)\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(-2{x}^{2}y-2x{y}^{2})\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,-2\\sqrt{5}({x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278480 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4272be20-e9f6-5f58-8d65-20ec49dcd88c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}({e}^{4}-1)\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276060} {:answer/id #uuid "6f9849ff-8b7c-559c-9004-711a9263b908" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(e-1)\\sqrt{2}\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276484} {:answer/id #uuid "875101c4-2932-5d0c-8dfc-50974a95eb38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276696} {:answer/id #uuid "8ea90df1-70bc-5a3d-bc73-2c004006066f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{4}\\sqrt{5}\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276772} {:answer/id #uuid "ec474ca4-06b0-5ff6-8cf7-8b695d4c2c54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({e}^{4}-1)\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277550}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4272be20-e9f6-5f58-8d65-20ec49dcd88c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}({e}^{4}-1)\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276060} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2c03fe3-db1e-5523-92c9-13f7cee7e078" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278151}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cedd414d-7a92-5f38-8d1c-98c03492493d" :question/math-academy-id "q-161322" :question/problem "![](images/92/9215246969b621002a96dec3aed8a012045b8061371e4c6a1234f54d53de29cc.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=x+\\sqrt{3}y$ whose domain is the circle of radius $2$ centered at $O,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+\\sqrt{3}y)=1,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+\\sqrt{3}y)=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+{1}^{2}+(\\sqrt{3})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}{∬}_{D}\\,{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5}{∬}_{D}\\,{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & =\\sqrt{5}{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}{∬}_{Δ}{e}^{({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}{∬}_{Δ}{e}^{{r}^{2}}r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}r{e}^{{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{[\\frac{{e}^{{r}^{2}}}{2}]}_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{{e}^{4}-1}{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}({e}^{4}-1)}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}({e}^{4}-1)}{2}\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}({e}^{4}-1)\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278481 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66ddc6b9-504b-58c8-bd2c-8ca80ec11d33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(6{x}^{4}+4{y}^{2}x+{y}^{4}{x}^{2})\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276397} {:answer/id #uuid "6ed6f32a-ed59-5647-bcd6-86e6da4ee2ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({x}^{4}-4{y}^{2}{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2})\\sqrt{1+16{x}^{2}-4{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276471} {:answer/id #uuid "9b1689bf-d1a3-5784-9e6f-9117c0c02c2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4{x}^{6}-4{y}^{2}{x}^{4}+4{y}^{4}x)\\sqrt{1+16{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276867} {:answer/id #uuid "bd9b2183-af1c-5150-ab05-abef28325ca3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({x}^{6}-4{y}^{2}{x}^{4}+4{y}^{4}{x}^{2})\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277169} {:answer/id #uuid "fb1e7756-7d83-53a3-b74f-dfc6cb5f4b38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({x}^{4}-{y}^{2}{x}^{3}+4{y}^{4}{x}^{2})\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}-4{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277679}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd9b2183-af1c-5150-ab05-abef28325ca3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({x}^{6}-4{y}^{2}{x}^{4}+4{y}^{4}{x}^{2})\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277169} :answer-field/id #uuid "60f5e341-969c-5ed5-9a8c-831fa22f5974" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277896}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "26332fd2-3603-5c3f-9cd0-0420c8d9d4db" :question/math-academy-id "q-161325" :question/problem "Consider the surface integral of the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}{z}^{2},$ where the surface $S$ is defined by $z=-{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}$ for $-1\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$\nThis surface integral can be written as a repeated integral, as shown below. \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2})=-2x,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2})=4y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(-2x)}^{2}+{(4y)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,{x}^{2}(-{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2})^{2}\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,({x}^{6}-4{y}^{2}{x}^{4}+4{y}^{4}{x}^{2})\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}}\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278482 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2be09bb0-84f0-526b-aafb-e6ef197cae15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275877} {:answer/id #uuid "34439043-1945-573c-a092-3245a91a3a5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275943} {:answer/id #uuid "82ddcb90-8ebf-5b75-be87-5b46f53239ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276655} {:answer/id #uuid "9896525e-34a1-55c7-9113-4dcd46fc1dca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276847} {:answer/id #uuid "e88b12c5-418b-52ca-a2d9-64992eaa7a6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277510}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "34439043-1945-573c-a092-3245a91a3a5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275943} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9772f5e-53c7-5b33-9a41-57e58832804a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278107}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d00a0fdd-b39f-55a4-9c73-ceedda035fbc" :question/math-academy-id "q-161330" :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{z}{\\int }_{0}^{y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y+z)\\,dx\\,dy\\,dz=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x,$ treating $y$ and $z$ as constants: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{z}{\\int }_{0}^{y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y+z)\\,dx\\,dy\\,dz & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{z}[{\\int }_{0}^{y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y+z)\\,dx]dy\\,dz \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{z}[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y+z)]_{x=0}^{x=y}\\,dy\\,dz \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{z}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y+y+z)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0+y+z)\\,dy\\,dz \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{z}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y+z)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y+z)\\,dy\\,dz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y,$ treating $z$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{z}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y+z)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y+z)\\,dy\\,dz & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}[{\\int }_{0}^{z}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y+z)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y+z)\\,dy]dz \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y+z)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y+z)]}_{y=0}^{y=z}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(z+z)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2z+z)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0+z)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0+z)\\,dz \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2z-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3z-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡z\\,dz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2z-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3z-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡z\\,dz & ={[-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2z+\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3z+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡z]}_{z=0}^{z=\\pi /2} \\\\  & =[-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi +\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})]-[-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡0] \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{2}+0+0]-[-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278485 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "030e15da-c3f2-5010-b17e-36a7ed473c2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275505} {:answer/id #uuid "3943bf20-6368-560d-b021-4fae192def24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275985} {:answer/id #uuid "b20e35c5-98d1-5c36-b505-ea104aa051fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277071} {:answer/id #uuid "c29949ec-ecba-572a-a5e3-61f0926ecd77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277208} {:answer/id #uuid "d0b00085-537a-5d3f-9fa4-c83ffd436789" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277323}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "030e15da-c3f2-5010-b17e-36a7ed473c2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275505} :answer-field/id #uuid "ddf19fb6-5605-5fb3-b7bb-92a36f76e8e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278117}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cb39b773-6ddf-5c8b-9d03-9af33b6087cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-161336" :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{y}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}z}\\frac{z}{{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,dx\\,dz\\,dy=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x,$ treating $y$ and $z$ as constants: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{y}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}z}\\frac{z}{{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,dx\\,dz\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{y}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}z}\\frac{z}{{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,dx]dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{y}{[z\\cdot \\frac{1}{z}\\cdot \\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{z})]}_{x=0}^{x=\\sqrt{3}z}\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{y}{[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{z})]}_{x=0}^{x=\\sqrt{3}z}\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{y}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\sqrt{3}-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{y}\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\,dz\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $z,$ treating $y$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{y}\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\,dz\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{y}\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\,dz]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[\\frac{\\pi }{3}{\\int }_{0}^{y}dz]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{\\pi }{3}[z]_{z=0}^{z=y}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\cdot y\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\,y\\,dy & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}{[\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}(2-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278486 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1117310d-a59f-5c9e-afac-07ea4e950362" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275629} {:answer/id #uuid "632694f6-bc89-55a5-9c76-587db4efb69f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276376} {:answer/id #uuid "71b9ff33-9fa7-56e6-aa27-7e58aa28fdea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276503} {:answer/id #uuid "c5f23306-38b6-5585-95c9-674db2d3737e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277238} {:answer/id #uuid "e6be6744-6027-57b8-8e56-b5d3f941e930" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277492}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1117310d-a59f-5c9e-afac-07ea4e950362" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275629} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3963dbe-4605-5d7a-9bdb-2239345ae704" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278095}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e935e93c-c375-5e87-b42b-951f2a795ef1" :question/math-academy-id "q-161339" :question/problem "![](images/d1/d19d54d0e76ced24d1bd9f865578b72f2b8d67414c8506499c6ae7b1300a6275.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=-2x+2y$ and whose domain is the left half-circle of radius $1$ centered at $O$, as shown above." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2x+2y)=-2,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2x+2y)=2.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(x+y)\\cdot \\sqrt{1+(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(x+y)\\cdot \\sqrt{9}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =3{∬}_{D}\\,(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{∬}_{D}\\,(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A & =3{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =3{∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{∬}_{Δ}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ){\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ){[\\frac{{r}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =[\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278487 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2da399a0-9004-55cb-b8d9-bfcba192e10f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275890} {:answer/id #uuid "35792833-1fb2-5e72-a052-4c2a08fe3ccd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275954} {:answer/id #uuid "63dad666-7ae8-5c0c-9304-710e5a18abda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276382} {:answer/id #uuid "ac6c526d-1bf9-5e51-840e-1fadba1e3733" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277026} {:answer/id #uuid "c586f151-6b60-5595-baef-e2fe4bca9d66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277234}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2da399a0-9004-55cb-b8d9-bfcba192e10f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275890} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4418da4-eada-53a0-ae03-28ed522c4f40" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278155}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5e393098-f8db-5718-a78a-3c0ee5f6534f" :question/math-academy-id "q-161340" :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{z}}\\,\\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=\\frac{1}{4}+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $-2\\le y\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{1}{4}+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2x,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{1}{4}+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be evaluated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{z}}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\sqrt{1+(2x)^{2}+(2y)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{2xy}{\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}}\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{2xy}{\\cancel{\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}}}\\cancel{\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,2xy\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-2}^{1}(2xy)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[x{y}^{2}]_{y=-2}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}(1-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278488 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58c92c9f-f7b2-5087-b5cb-9c823dd91103" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276275} {:answer/id #uuid "71e45d75-8e8d-5b51-9bc8-9a9063a27f9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276506} {:answer/id #uuid "7547c285-1634-5801-b577-f69f0bcd9c1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276540} {:answer/id #uuid "c5f4f348-c70a-58b1-b1c6-5b7181818124" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277239} {:answer/id #uuid "c82c5022-5c05-5a6e-8240-b77817d39cb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277257}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71e45d75-8e8d-5b51-9bc8-9a9063a27f9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276506} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d30624d-c8e2-5de7-a21a-9e4e44de6e77" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277849}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b54f01b4-5da4-54d3-8529-0362ccff055b" :question/math-academy-id "q-161341" :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}4z\\,\\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=x+y$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+y)=1,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+y)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be evaluated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}4z\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,4(x+y)\\,\\sqrt{1+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}4(x+y)\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[4xy+2{y}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(4x+2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{[2{x}^{2}+2x]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278489 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12b8122a-dfab-5145-bbd8-24d92990a44a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275646} {:answer/id #uuid "889d9d49-96a4-5779-b95f-be15fa5078d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276710} {:answer/id #uuid "8b0fa420-8dc1-5c7b-8e47-218b01d4969f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276736} {:answer/id #uuid "ad6d0b17-766e-5080-b0b9-6aaa1c85c4d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277036} {:answer/id #uuid "d99ad1ba-4be5-5cec-9301-a6bfe0768270" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277387}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "889d9d49-96a4-5779-b95f-be15fa5078d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276710} :answer-field/id #uuid "ddb38f1f-b709-52d6-832e-5b18b3f8d536" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278115}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6a641492-289d-52ec-861c-68bcfc571351" :question/math-academy-id "q-161344" :question/problem "${\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{{z}^{2}}\\frac{x}{z}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{y}{z})\\,dy\\,dz\\,dx=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y,$ treating $x$ and $z$ as constants: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{{z}^{2}}\\frac{x}{z}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{y}{z})\\,dy\\,dz\\,dx & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}[{\\int }_{0}^{{z}^{2}}\\frac{x}{z}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{y}{z})\\,dy]dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{[\\frac{x}{z}\\cdot z\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{y}{z})]}_{y=0}^{y={z}^{2}}\\,dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}x{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{y}{z})]}_{y=0}^{y={z}^{2}}\\,dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}x(\\operatorname{sin}⁡z-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0)\\,dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡z\\,dz\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $z,$ treating $x$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡z\\,dz\\,dx & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡z\\,dz]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}[-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡z]_{z=0}^{z=\\pi /2}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}x(-0+1)\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}x\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}x\\,dx & ={[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{x=-1}^{x=0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(0-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278490 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c3d0569-d187-59ac-bbee-efb695007c16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275581} {:answer/id #uuid "3296f945-ab43-5da2-a325-5e08838b6854" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275925} {:answer/id #uuid "7b91a9aa-767f-5b97-930d-5ca1f5d1aeef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276598} {:answer/id #uuid "9af42160-5f11-54ca-b2fb-69251af372f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276866} {:answer/id #uuid "eb357f0d-d040-5500-a756-d81d745e7358" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277532}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb357f0d-d040-5500-a756-d81d745e7358" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277532} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fa7068b-f628-57ab-b2e1-bd150d239161" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277948}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "48f0648b-6535-5240-b098-b0f79d4f5777" :question/math-academy-id "q-161347" :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\frac{z-y}{\\sqrt{6}y}\\,\\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=2x+y$ for $1\\le x\\le 2$ and $1\\le y\\le e.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x+y)=2,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x+y)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be evaluated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\frac{z-y}{\\sqrt{6}y}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{(2x+y)-y}{\\sqrt{6}y}\\sqrt{1+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{2x}{y}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{2x}{y}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}2x[\\operatorname{ln}⁡y]_{y=1}^{y=e}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[{x}^{2}]_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =4-1 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278491 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fb776aa-a231-5b67-b512-2e48959b2c35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275612} {:answer/id #uuid "2c72c2e2-f9da-516e-8437-c3433bda72fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275878} {:answer/id #uuid "6b245dba-d742-5bc5-8638-3bc2d09ec996" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "26\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276438} {:answer/id #uuid "e708156e-7ac2-5ba9-a3f4-3d08a688734d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5+\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277493} {:answer/id #uuid "fa7e67ad-f360-562e-a7fa-616dede524b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25+\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277668}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b245dba-d742-5bc5-8638-3bc2d09ec996" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "26\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276438} :answer-field/id #uuid "7cc0abcd-c9b6-5dbe-9576-78ef1cbd7653" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277945}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4c18475c-c837-5db2-8109-d064b9976225" :question/math-academy-id "q-161348" :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}({x}^{2}+{z}^{2})\\,\\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=5x-y$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 2x.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(5x-y)=5,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(5x-y)=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be evaluated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}({x}^{2}+{z}^{2})\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,({x}^{2}+(5x-y)^{2})\\sqrt{1+{5}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,({x}^{2}+25{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-10xy)\\sqrt{27}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{D}\\,(26{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-10xy)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2x}(26{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-10xy)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[26{x}^{2}y+\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}-5x{y}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(52{x}^{3}+\\frac{8{x}^{3}}{3}-20{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{104{x}^{3}}{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}104{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}[26{x}^{4}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =26\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278492 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02f8c6b6-9cbe-58bf-8e1a-5bd0d46273c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275503} {:answer/id #uuid "0f5d63cd-8e10-5ad9-a3a3-f0c06c319be3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275609} {:answer/id #uuid "4cb403ed-339e-5d6e-a57d-22f54539edb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276154} {:answer/id #uuid "78e8a8f1-a8e1-575e-adb9-80cd272126a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276574} {:answer/id #uuid "9b8581a2-7002-504b-b509-61205105060a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276872}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b8581a2-7002-504b-b509-61205105060a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276872} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d9bfea1-60f3-55d1-aee8-4fb4c6843eb8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277799}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0921ef67-5139-5147-a7ee-44b4f995ddab" :question/math-academy-id "q-161352" :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\frac{z}{36x}\\,\\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=4x+8y$ for $1\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le x.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x+8y)=4,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4x+8y)=8.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be evaluated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\frac{z}{36x}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{4x+8y}{36x}\\sqrt{1+{4}^{2}+{8}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,9\\frac{4(x+2y)}{36x}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{x+2y}{x}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}(1+\\frac{2y}{x})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{[y+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x}]}_{0}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(x+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x}-0)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[{x}^{2}]_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =4-1 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278493 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35f5cd1a-63bc-5976-8e15-1113f79de020" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275958} {:answer/id #uuid "38102cb9-5485-5c2d-bfd7-d76de819dfdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275972} {:answer/id #uuid "7bdbf9a4-5472-55cb-97f0-e433a1aaca64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276605} {:answer/id #uuid "a4db42e4-f7e4-58d2-b12a-3b3284811363" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276962} {:answer/id #uuid "ec6c3115-1b4b-5dac-9190-2c35f60c7815" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277551}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7bdbf9a4-5472-55cb-97f0-e433a1aaca64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276605} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f5eb094-750a-507e-bd70-60dfcf8aba05" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277923}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "70e559da-f697-5786-a70f-285bfb1e201a" :question/math-academy-id "q-161353" :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=1+x+y$ for $0\\le x\\le \\frac{1}{2}$ and $-x\\le y\\le 0.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1+x+y)=1,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1+x+y)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be evaluated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}(1+x+y)^{2}}\\sqrt{1+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}{\\int }_{-x}^{0}\\,\\frac{1}{(1+x+y)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}{[-\\frac{1}{1+x+y}]}_{y=-x}^{y=0}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}(-\\frac{1}{1+x}+1)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+x]_{0}^{1/2} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})+\\frac{1}{2}-0 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278511 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02096d36-ef67-5df8-a1fc-77f61f4fb7fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{xz}\\,$ and ${x}^{2}+{z}^{2}=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275495} {:answer/id #uuid "0890c454-a982-5e24-83e4-ccc175ff3e85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{1+\\frac{x+z}{{x}^{4}{z}^{4}}}\\,$ and ${x}^{2}+{z}^{2}=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275553} {:answer/id #uuid "c58299d5-6fa2-5896-8f16-ff7a78529fc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}{xz}\\,$ and ${x}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277233} {:answer/id #uuid "df5bf9db-65c0-5560-b70c-3b38da48a945" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}}{{x}^{4}{z}^{4}}}\\,$ and ${x}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277431} {:answer/id #uuid "e618a500-fbdb-51bb-b479-a405e95937de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{1+\\frac{x+z}{{x}^{2}{z}^{2}}}\\,$ and ${x}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\ge 2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277487}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df5bf9db-65c0-5560-b70c-3b38da48a945" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}}{{x}^{4}{z}^{4}}}\\,$ and ${x}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277431} :answer-field/id #uuid "55ee5d51-e4ff-5d64-b516-e18fbea43931" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277876}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1e50892b-6dbf-5e7c-ae2e-f87a4a349f42" :question/math-academy-id "q-161443" :question/problem "$$\n{∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A,\\,D=\\{(x,z)\\,:\\,\\boxed{}\\,\\}\n$$\n\nThe integral above gives the surface area of the part of the surface $y=\\frac{1}{xz}$ that lies inside the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{z}^{2}=2.$ From left to right, what are the missing expressions?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $y=f(x,z)$ with $(x,z)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{xz},\\,{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 2.\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂y}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{1}{xz})=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}z}, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\frac{1}{xz})=-\\frac{1}{x{z}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}z})}^{2}+{(-\\frac{1}{x{z}^{2}})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}{z}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}{z}^{4}}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}}{{x}^{4}{z}^{4}}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is\n\n$\\,$ $\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}}{{x}^{4}{z}^{4}}}\\,$ and ${x}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278532 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "201eeb86-71f6-5d21-8a07-77b67d4fe0b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275776} {:answer/id #uuid "4b4709d1-f20d-55f8-8e11-03a64466deb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276140} {:answer/id #uuid "4cc991a7-cba4-5355-bb0e-f908a28ca6c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276155} {:answer/id #uuid "8d8dd1b5-5197-5da1-934f-b16113545a8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276760} {:answer/id #uuid "d2dc1cf4-470e-5a1a-8707-897ecce9ec5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277338}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4cc991a7-cba4-5355-bb0e-f908a28ca6c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276155} :answer-field/id #uuid "5043bbaf-f0ca-58b0-b6a2-528528b9a9f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277860}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4169910a-ce7d-5d1b-9b0e-97a83be3a13e" :question/math-academy-id "q-161500" :question/problem "Evaluate the triple integral \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}16x\\,dV\n$$\n\nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,0\\le z\\le x-y},\n$$\n\nand $D$ is a finite region in $xy$-plane enclosed between the coordinate axes and the line $y=2x-3$ in the fourth quadrant." :question/worked-solution "Writing down our triple integral as a mixed integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}16x\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{x-y}16x\\,dz]dA.\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $z,$ treating $x$ and $y$ as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{x-y}16x\\,dz]dA & ={∬}_{D}[16xz]_{z=0}^{z=x-y}\\,dA \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}16x(x-y)\\,dA\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe must now evaluate the double integral above over the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane shown below.\n\n![](images/bd/bddbd1c97fe8cd74f9903dec340495f04401323b8e1236680c57c13b18069eb4.png)\n\nWe can evaluate our double integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}16x(x-y)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{3/2}{\\int }_{2x-3}^{0}16x(x-y)\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3/2}16x[{\\int }_{2x-3}^{0}(x-y)\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3/2}16x{[xy-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}]}_{y=2x-3}^{y=0}dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3/2}16x(0-x(2x-3)+\\frac{1}{2}(2x-3)^{2})\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3/2}8x(-4{x}^{2}+6x+4{x}^{2}-12x+9)\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3/2}8x(-6x+9)\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3/2}-48{x}^{2}+72x\\,dx \\\\  & ={[-16{x}^{3}+36{x}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=3/2} \\\\  & =-16{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{3}+36{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =-54+81 \\\\  & =27\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278546 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55b0b9b9-6c61-52a0-883d-72dabb5dc5f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276237} {:answer/id #uuid "70f28e28-c030-5053-a069-7bc3877eb33b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276493} {:answer/id #uuid "80ad5cf6-f9ff-56a1-bcb6-20cadcc92134" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276642} {:answer/id #uuid "ca8eb565-31a6-5155-8e44-4a4d6a7de0dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277276} {:answer/id #uuid "fce9c2cc-cb68-5fd2-aff3-f988333f015d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277693}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55b0b9b9-6c61-52a0-883d-72dabb5dc5f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276237} :answer-field/id #uuid "5439730e-b587-501e-b283-51c217f9d9d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277869}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d38df384-c529-5d23-b152-2f8b3a09208a" :question/math-academy-id "q-161539" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨{cos}^{2}⁡(xy),\\,{cos}^{2}⁡(xy),\\,1⟩,\n$$\n\nwhere the surface $S$ is defined by $z=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(xy)$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $-x\\le y\\le 0.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(xy))=\\frac{y}{{cos}^{2}⁡(xy)} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(xy))=\\frac{x}{{cos}^{2}⁡(xy)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the flux of $F$ through $S$ is given by \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨{cos}^{2}⁡(xy),\\,{cos}^{2}⁡(xy),\\,1⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y,g(x,y)) & =⟨{cos}^{2}⁡(xy),\\,{cos}^{2}⁡(xy),\\,1⟩{|}_{z=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(xy)} \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{{cos}^{2}⁡(xy)}_{P},\\,\\underbrace{{cos}^{2}⁡(xy)}_{Q},\\,\\underbrace{1}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate our surface integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨{cos}^{2}⁡(xy),\\,{cos}^{2}⁡(xy),\\,1⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-({cos}^{2}⁡(xy))\\cdot (\\frac{y}{{cos}^{2}⁡(xy)})-({cos}^{2}⁡(xy))\\cdot (\\frac{x}{{cos}^{2}⁡(xy)})+1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-y-x+1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-x}^{0}(-y-x+1)\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}-xy+y]}_{y=-x}^{y=0}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-{x}^{2}+x)\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+x)\\,dx \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281292 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "546af882-b367-56aa-b818-8192ce2828bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279359} {:answer/id #uuid "b84ac7bc-b2e1-5cea-985b-2954b342f53a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280214} {:answer/id #uuid "cd9adf2c-2a98-58c5-8fd7-fb367df94817" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280406} {:answer/id #uuid "d863e60c-d243-5dcd-a905-644d527833af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280503} {:answer/id #uuid "fba41283-2e57-54cb-b312-b32d1917aee4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280796}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fba41283-2e57-54cb-b312-b32d1917aee4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280796} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0016591-9999-5fe2-ae9e-6b503dd172a4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281198}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7a372c14-9ab9-5f40-9e05-8b6bd0720902" :question/math-academy-id "q-161677" :question/problem "Find the volume of the solid region enclosed by the $xy$-plane, the surface $z=4-{x}^{2},$ and the planes $x=0,$ $x=2,$ $y=0,$ $y=2.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our solid can be written as a type I region: \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 4-{x}^{2}\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of $R$ is given by the following triple integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4-{x}^{2}}\\,dz\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[z]_{z=0}^{z=4-{x}^{2}}\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}4-{x}^{2}\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(4-{x}^{2})[y]_{y=0}^{y=2}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(4-{x}^{2})(2-0)\\,dx \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}4-{x}^{2}\\,dx \\\\  & =2{[4x-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}]}_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =2(4(2)-\\frac{1}{3}({2}^{3}))-0 \\\\  & =2(\\frac{16}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281318 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "258dafab-1312-54cc-a1aa-d8bca4a4997e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278925} {:answer/id #uuid "4c81601a-173b-521e-9ffe-213cef29a4fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279279} {:answer/id #uuid "8c870b65-1c61-5e6e-8d41-46f31e8128d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279844} {:answer/id #uuid "8f79aaea-bd49-511b-8193-9928b3d7a4ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279867} {:answer/id #uuid "e066abea-4387-591c-a343-6bad532178f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280574}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f79aaea-bd49-511b-8193-9928b3d7a4ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279867} :answer-field/id #uuid "50eeabe8-5913-5f86-93fe-a762dc1ea709" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280962}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "266f9785-a539-521c-9afc-7ee59882e02f" :question/math-academy-id "q-161776" :question/problem "Evaluate the triple integral \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}3x\\,dV\n$$\n\nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 4},\n$$\n\nand $D$ is a finite region in $xz$-plane enclosed between the $x$-axis, the $z$-axis, and the curve $z+{x}^{2}=4$ in the first quadrant." :question/worked-solution "Writing down our triple integral as a mixed integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}3x\\,dV={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{4}3x\\,dy]dA.\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $y,$ treating $x$ and $z$ as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{4}3x\\,dy]dA & ={∬}_{D}3x[y]_{y={x}^{2}}^{y=4}\\,dA \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3x(4-{x}^{2})\\,dA\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe must now evaluate this double integral over the region $D$ in the $xz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](images/bc/bcac3bb634fa04d2ccd4c3897f7b1db891a0b21eacfa96579f672b812fd92c07.png)\n\n \n\nWe can evaluate our double integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}3x(4-{x}^{2})\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4-{x}^{2}}3x(4-{x}^{2})\\,dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}3x(4-{x}^{2})[z]_{z=0}^{z=4-{x}^{2}}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}3x(4-{x}^{2})(4-{x}^{2})\\,dx \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(-2x)(4-{x}^{2})^{2}\\,dx \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}{[\\frac{(4-{x}^{2})^{3}}{3}]}_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}(\\frac{0-{4}^{3}}{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}(-\\frac{64}{3}) \\\\  & =32\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281320 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e7db8c3-bef0-5a3e-a87b-4c83f7590a99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{6},\\,\\frac{4\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278877} {:answer/id #uuid "30e26a0b-d8c9-57b4-b05e-f879bae39613" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{6},\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279018} {:answer/id #uuid "89b9e00e-39c3-5783-a7f2-743d0e73d055" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3},\\,\\frac{11\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279818} {:answer/id #uuid "9ce02394-0bc8-532e-b532-1d7105cce9f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{6},\\,\\frac{11\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279988} {:answer/id #uuid "e64272d6-f3c8-57a4-baa3-00c44183914b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3},\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280622}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ce02394-0bc8-532e-b532-1d7105cce9f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{6},\\,\\frac{11\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279988} :answer-field/id #uuid "a90e9a21-97e6-56dc-a2f3-66166409b19f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281127}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "20bf336b-caa8-55e7-b9e9-31b0b8d95086" :question/math-academy-id "q-16178" :question/problem "Find the values of $\\theta $ at which the curve $x=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi $ intersects the line $\\sqrt{3}x+y=0.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the coordinates of the points of intersection, we take the equation of the line, replace $x$ and $y$ with the parametric formulas, and then solve for $\\theta :$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}x+y & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}(\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe principal value of $\\theta $ is\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nThis value lies outside the domain $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .$ To get a value that's inside the domain, we add $\\pi :$\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\pi =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nTo get a second solution, we add $\\pi $ to ${\\theta }_{1}:$\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}+\\pi =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our solutions are $\\frac{5\\pi }{6},\\,\\frac{11\\pi }{6}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281329 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18d889e7-c824-5206-ae1f-be2ca84ac201" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278809} {:answer/id #uuid "54eae97a-126b-5169-968a-945a94267a89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279365} {:answer/id #uuid "ad2bc2ed-1ff1-5cb1-beed-8d5a3ea43e3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280124} {:answer/id #uuid "b815cee2-db42-5d6f-beef-5073c80b1726" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280211} {:answer/id #uuid "feaf148b-7dd0-5012-95f0-0e1627a40f3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280819}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54eae97a-126b-5169-968a-945a94267a89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279365} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a2c215b-9be4-534d-b695-b69d79a8eec9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280877}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "867c4c3a-9ff3-5bda-af78-c2d51483fcb4" :question/math-academy-id "q-161798" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)),$ given that \n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=z,\\,w=4y-2{z}^{2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =4 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =-4z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -4z\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -4z\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(0-4) \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-4)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281330 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06b82b5c-a4bc-5662-ada6-dda7518ac8c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278655} {:answer/id #uuid "0fc85f22-4a58-5c43-9914-7b18675f3668" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278731} {:answer/id #uuid "413c7a3a-e6cb-5e3d-b351-58d589d29f9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279171} {:answer/id #uuid "7cce492a-e940-5ea6-a555-ee7f7af4f9ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279706} {:answer/id #uuid "ffff14cf-d01e-51fb-a239-eecaf7d789e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280833}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0fc85f22-4a58-5c43-9914-7b18675f3668" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278731} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd07e6bc-736c-579e-90c2-ab826af79391" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281217}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f9050d05-b087-5029-bf1c-8747b808c301" :question/math-academy-id "q-161800" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)),$ given that \n\n$$\nu=z-y,\\,v=x+z,\\,w=-3x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =-3 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(-1)\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{vmatrix}+1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(0+3)+1(0-0) \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281331 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c2caa68-f00a-5537-8bd9-f6cb76c877ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279273} {:answer/id #uuid "600dfa31-4e37-5a16-ba48-c5ca10f9fbf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279473} {:answer/id #uuid "b204a652-49ca-5415-ba78-58814749d96e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280165} {:answer/id #uuid "c706e56c-9384-5437-a653-797286bbede3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280336} {:answer/id #uuid "fa5dc6c7-df6e-5fdb-b0fa-59f45de8fd17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280789}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa5dc6c7-df6e-5fdb-b0fa-59f45de8fd17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280789} :answer-field/id #uuid "22d4e8c1-df31-56ff-9384-be31853aa6b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280891}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4f0a4eb6-83e4-58b7-81d7-e22ca020da04" :question/math-academy-id "q-161801" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)),$ given that \n\n$$\nu=y-3z,\\,v=x+2z,\\,w=3x-y.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =-3 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =3 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix}-3\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-1(0-6)-3(-1-0) \\\\  & =6+3 \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281332 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b6cc79b-3207-598f-8d70-80b25ee5e718" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278846} {:answer/id #uuid "a3d99844-e42f-56e8-86a6-5d5458f5fa76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280045} {:answer/id #uuid "a4c82ac3-d2d1-560d-a1c4-451dfa40fa91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280052} {:answer/id #uuid "cc93804e-fbe9-508c-968d-dea27a808ace" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280388} {:answer/id #uuid "f373368b-6b80-5e55-91f1-61c0a4bf9baf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280720}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f373368b-6b80-5e55-91f1-61c0a4bf9baf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280720} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff6d4ed4-6f38-58ef-90e7-56012b55823a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281283}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "78cdb0a8-90d4-5ce3-9ce4-dfaf056d437e" :question/math-academy-id "q-161802" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)),$ given that \n\n$$\nu=3x+yz,\\,v=z,\\,w=-y.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =3 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =z & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =y \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & z & y \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3(0+1) \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281334 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b44cea0-ad33-5976-923f-1f474b30b292" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{u+v+w}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278975} {:answer/id #uuid "460aadea-67c4-543b-8250-5f0974161a89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{u+v+w}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279217} {:answer/id #uuid "5d4a44db-5da9-5c22-8623-1c9463ab6122" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{uvw}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279447} {:answer/id #uuid "b93d3489-33b8-59f0-b4e7-ed465bfeb6ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u+v+w" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280221} {:answer/id #uuid "ebb87757-2b77-5b42-9577-c7fa19032c21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{uvw}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280664}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ebb87757-2b77-5b42-9577-c7fa19032c21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{uvw}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280664} :answer-field/id #uuid "00162f26-ff18-503b-9399-9d89f9b3560f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280834}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "256171d6-afaa-51cd-ae04-b8a4f7e24f09" :question/math-academy-id "q-161804" :question/problem "Consider the transformation $T:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y,z)\\to (u(x,y,z),v(x,y,z),w(x,y,z))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{x} \\\\ {e}^{x+y} \\\\ {e}^{x+y+z}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T.$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nu(x,y,z)={e}^{x},\\,v(x,y,z)={e}^{x+y},\\,w(x,y,z)={e}^{x+y+z}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & ={e}^{x} & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & ={e}^{x+y} & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & ={e}^{x+y} & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & ={e}^{x+y+z} & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & ={e}^{x+y+z} & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & ={e}^{x+y+z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{x} & 0 & 0 \\\\ {e}^{x+y} & {e}^{x+y} & 0 \\\\ {e}^{x+y+z} & {e}^{x+y+z} & {e}^{x+y+z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{x+y} & 0 \\\\ {e}^{x+y+z} & {e}^{x+y+z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}({e}^{2x+2y+z}-0) \\\\  & ={e}^{3x+2y+z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting down $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}$ in terms of $u,$ $v,$ and $w,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3x+2y+z} & ={e}^{x+(x+y)+(x+y+z)} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\cdot {e}^{x+y}\\cdot {e}^{x+y+z} \\\\  & =uvw\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{uvw}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281335 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d44b6cc-3a93-5117-a27f-6f280b9b11c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{u}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278862} {:answer/id #uuid "47b2751a-712d-5998-bd6d-e5e60e8c5b22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{v+w}{u}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279233} {:answer/id #uuid "7a98c4b3-ed46-5cfd-8a6c-e55f7a475d3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(w-v)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279683} {:answer/id #uuid "9aa88b3c-e8a4-5165-97f7-0e1e8c3eec3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "uv" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279962} {:answer/id #uuid "f792ff52-2762-5037-adc2-ea50c01f1006" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280762}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d44b6cc-3a93-5117-a27f-6f280b9b11c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{u}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278862} :answer-field/id #uuid "db389b2e-8734-58ef-b276-82b4a342763c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281214}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "21d2a60a-f1f7-5e79-815f-2e9babe829a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-161805" :question/problem "Consider the transformation $T:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y,z)\\to (u(x,y,z),v(x,y,z),w(x,y,z))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ z-y \\\\ xy\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T.$\n\n*Hint: You can assume that $x\\ne 0.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nu(x,y,z)=x,\\,v(x,y,z)=z-y,\\,w(x,y,z)=xy.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =y & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =x & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ y & x & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ x & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(0-x) \\\\  & =-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting down $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}$ in terms of $u,$ $v,$ and $w,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x & =-u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-u)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{u}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281336 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21988933-2fdd-5343-979b-14ba2f6e68d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{uvw}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278894} {:answer/id #uuid "413d8651-c816-595c-bfd1-f51fa29900be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{uvw}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279172} {:answer/id #uuid "4e964f3b-c23d-5355-8277-f886ef034e49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{uvw}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279306} {:answer/id #uuid "7bc53c83-70d3-5bd8-adc4-31458e829c60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4uvw" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279694} {:answer/id #uuid "8888de73-401c-58c0-8c74-d084f5008000" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{uvw}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279811}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "413d8651-c816-595c-bfd1-f51fa29900be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{uvw}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279172} :answer-field/id #uuid "efb87408-7532-5806-bc11-5c1c98d338e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281257}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9bd3e29c-50b2-5640-b3fa-d0adf89e1fac" :question/math-academy-id "q-161806" :question/problem "Consider the transformation $T:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y,z)\\to (u(x,y,z),v(x,y,z),w(x,y,z))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}xy \\\\ yz \\\\ xz\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T.$\n\n*Hint: You can assume that $x,y,z>0.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nu(x,y,z)=xy,\\,v(x,y,z)=yz,\\,w(x,y,z)=xz.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =y & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =x & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =z & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =y \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =z & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}y & x & 0 \\\\ 0 & z & y \\\\ z & 0 & x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =y\\begin{vmatrix}z & y \\\\ 0 & x\\end{vmatrix}-x\\begin{vmatrix}0 & y \\\\ z & x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =y(xz-0)-x(0-yz) \\\\  & =xyz+xyz \\\\  & =2xyz.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting down $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}$ in terms of $u,$ $v,$ and $w,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2xyz & =2\\sqrt{(xyz)^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{xy(yz)(xz)} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{uvw}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{uvw}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281337 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0626020c-ffd0-5d52-be60-af389fc83a02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(uvw)^{1/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278648} {:answer/id #uuid "57bde7fd-ba02-508b-9c77-edf62367e8ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9(uvw)^{2/3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279398} {:answer/id #uuid "84467733-f429-5feb-8388-07e2d51f176e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(uvw)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279778} {:answer/id #uuid "af416bf2-0b93-5782-b6de-1f1c2d402fc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(uvw)^{2/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280143} {:answer/id #uuid "c8f31297-6e1d-54c7-be43-6fdd25ab29fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{(uvw)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280352}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57bde7fd-ba02-508b-9c77-edf62367e8ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9(uvw)^{2/3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279398} :answer-field/id #uuid "2bbffbbf-30e0-555b-b5e7-55199b3bfffb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280913}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "82aa7a5f-aede-5ec9-8984-8cdea4eb6f95" :question/math-academy-id "q-161807" :question/problem "Consider the transformation $T:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y,z)\\to (u(x,y,z),v(x,y,z),w(x,y,z))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}y{z}^{2} \\\\ z{x}^{2} \\\\ x{y}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T.$\n\n*Hint: You can assume that $x,y,z\\ne 0.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nu(x,y,z)=y{z}^{2},\\,v(x,y,z)=z{x}^{2},\\,w(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & ={z}^{2} & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =2yz \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =2xz & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & ={y}^{2} & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =2xy & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & {z}^{2} & 2yz \\\\ 2xz & 0 & {x}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2} & 2xy & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-{z}^{2}\\begin{vmatrix}2xy & {x}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2} & 0\\end{vmatrix}+2yz\\begin{vmatrix}2xz & 0 \\\\ {y}^{2} & 2xy\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-{z}^{2}(0-{x}^{2}{y}^{2})+2yz(4{x}^{2}yz-0) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+8{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2} \\\\  & =9{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting down $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}$ in terms of $u,$ $v,$ and $w,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2} & =9{({x}^{3}{y}^{3}{z}^{3})}^{2/3} \\\\  & =9{(y{z}^{2}\\cdot z{x}^{2}\\cdot x{y}^{2})}^{2/3} \\\\  & =9(uvw)^{2/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9(uvw)^{2/3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281353 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e5ece0f-ace5-59e3-8bac-5e7030391f49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/fe/fe7bb422a3f992ea88a900e63b2c55df99bce3a785929901c248abadfadf23f1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278998} {:answer/id #uuid "56614d5f-f51d-520c-bd5a-2efe188329bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/30/3033f0ae73e3f484fc5d3d65e388062feba1824fb3da9971d560cf2f518f49bb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279385} {:answer/id #uuid "63c73c6f-8214-55ad-9f4a-739379cccb77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/cb/cb5b22ff46569b231d122c2ed11bd412907f36271a033d86769435adf9fe48c7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279499} {:answer/id #uuid "90ce74d2-cefd-5461-a622-b31141a5968a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/13/1368ee41963c1abcc320d2cd35449c08599582f37c7d68f750e137fcdcd2e57b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279883} {:answer/id #uuid "df833e78-3be8-5410-bdd9-e871a352bba6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d4/d477a9f312fc2fb0a5a4c2d13164c96118aa70b53cf23db80be9ec698151e6fd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280563}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "90ce74d2-cefd-5461-a622-b31141a5968a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/13/1368ee41963c1abcc320d2cd35449c08599582f37c7d68f750e137fcdcd2e57b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279883} :answer-field/id #uuid "dfbed5de-5d8f-51b2-a9bc-07694301ed70" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281224}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "92a30208-5e48-56e6-a327-0e416b1fdedd" :question/math-academy-id "q-161842" :question/problem "Which of the following shows the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $T,$ given by \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{u}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} \\\\ u-\\frac{1}{3}v\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nis (locally) orientation-reversing?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing at the points where the Jacobian determinant is negative:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}<0\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{u}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} \\\\ u-\\frac{1}{3}v\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3{u}^{2} & -v \\\\ 1 & -\\frac{1}{3}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(3{u}^{2})\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3})-(1)\\cdot (-v) \\\\  & =v-{u}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v-{u}^{2} & <0 \\\\ v & <{u}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that the equation \n\n$$\nv={u}^{2}\n$$\n\ndefines a parabola, and the coordinates of, say, $(1,-1)$ satisfy the inequality. \n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing must be the following:\n\n![](images/13/1368ee41963c1abcc320d2cd35449c08599582f37c7d68f750e137fcdcd2e57b.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281366 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a27b376-f76e-5224-bd14-dcd0f58ef17d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279419} {:answer/id #uuid "6df88852-e281-540b-9be8-5da79ff04db5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279592} {:answer/id #uuid "e29c7966-53cc-5a98-a45d-f216ac316386" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280594} {:answer/id #uuid "e5bf82bf-7ae4-51b0-8078-b62137d694c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280619} {:answer/id #uuid "f2ae113f-b190-5e88-a500-9aa18217b4af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280706}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6df88852-e281-540b-9be8-5da79ff04db5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279592} :answer-field/id #uuid "150f9199-b68c-5aef-8efe-e079ecbd49f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280869}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9ee4cd0d-00ad-5fb0-be36-91eca3f6f7a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-161873" :question/problem "Consider a small rectangle $Δ$ whose bottom-left corner is located at the point $(2,2)$ with sides parallel to the coordinate axes in the $uv$-plane and whose area is $\\delta A.$ Which of the following gives the best approximation for the area of the image of this rectangle under the action of the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{u}-v \\\\ {u}^{2}v\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))≈|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\cdot \\underbrace{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}} & -1 \\\\ 2uv & {u}^{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}})\\cdot ({u}^{2})-2uv\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-1+2uv \\\\ {\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(2,2)} & =(-1+2uv)\\,{|}_{u=2\\,v=2} \\\\  & =-1+2(2)(2) \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & ≈|7|\\cdot \\delta A \\\\  & =7\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281371 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6868218d-e1cc-5d50-a993-c991f43b1d4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279541} {:answer/id #uuid "7cc9479c-c9e8-5ef9-bf90-29e06a33263c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279705} {:answer/id #uuid "9162be54-7854-53e2-9565-c82bf2b82b2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279888} {:answer/id #uuid "d5ff9956-5830-5df1-9e06-3627aaf262d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280480} {:answer/id #uuid "d96ff063-ffb7-5f8f-95e7-79745e683d0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280513}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6868218d-e1cc-5d50-a993-c991f43b1d4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279541} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf437f98-16a0-57d1-a983-d2d8546f1bd5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281197}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4a48d509-ce9f-57c3-ab71-29eb65410f8f" :question/math-academy-id "q-161885" :question/problem "Consider a small rectangle $Δ$ whose bottom-left corner is located at the point $(2,4)$ with sides parallel to the coordinate axes in the $uv$-plane and whose area is $\\delta A.$ Which of the following gives the best approximation for the area of the image of this rectangle under the action of the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{u}^{2}}{v} \\\\ uv\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))≈|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\cdot \\underbrace{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2u}{v} & -\\frac{{u}^{2}}{{v}^{2}} \\\\ v & u\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{2u}{v}\\cdot u-v\\cdot (-\\frac{{u}^{2}}{{v}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2{u}^{2}}{v}+\\frac{{u}^{2}}{v} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{u}^{2}}{v} \\\\ {\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(2,4)} & =(\\frac{3{u}^{2}}{v})\\,{|}_{u=2\\,v=4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(2)^{2}}{4} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & ≈|3|\\cdot \\delta A \\\\  & =3\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281375 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46218ce4-ea58-5de1-86c0-d1c69e873ba0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279220} {:answer/id #uuid "4f46e641-3ee3-5376-98dd-a02b81e26818" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{y-x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279312} {:answer/id #uuid "7d9b5b14-4c9b-524f-9436-0ac569357f98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{xy-y}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279717} {:answer/id #uuid "a8d59bc9-b571-564a-bff6-8c35092d9426" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+y}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280086} {:answer/id #uuid "cc67d8e2-c014-5128-9067-69e0d0ab8a73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+xy}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280386}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cc67d8e2-c014-5128-9067-69e0d0ab8a73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+xy}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280386} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ddaedb2-b68c-5509-aca4-917bce34c4eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280859}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a4e1f048-4c99-5435-999b-508c95c11d59" :question/math-academy-id "q-161899" :question/problem "![](images/8a/8a48be0b4c101babf157a05959e923576ad5614113b3eec9e93fe747d5dc3803.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨1,\\,x,\\,0⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAAAA}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So, \n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=0$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper hemisphere ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=1,\\,Q=x,\\,R=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-1\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}})-x\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}})+0\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x+xy}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower plane ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is downward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,-1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨1,x,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,-1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}0\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x+xy}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A+0 \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x+xy}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281380 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d4ab3c7-4edc-5bcf-a3c7-c2ee3b264e76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{y}^{3}+y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278863} {:answer/id #uuid "5283dd25-1966-5979-b060-46f14bb2261b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y{x}^{2}+2{y}^{3}+y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279342} {:answer/id #uuid "7ec41431-a0c9-5533-97af-87fa916bafd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y{x}^{2}-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279735} {:answer/id #uuid "c374e37e-c1ba-575f-ae04-b42bbeb06f9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y{x}^{2}+2{y}^{3}-3y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280311} {:answer/id #uuid "ed729a0a-c608-581b-a406-124015551a79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2y{x}^{2}-2{y}^{3}-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280674}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5283dd25-1966-5979-b060-46f14bb2261b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y{x}^{2}+2{y}^{3}+y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279342} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c07c295-70c7-54cd-ac02-143f145e1f60" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281091}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "82300a07-b333-5d6e-9b87-61998889d7c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-161914" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=⟨0,\\,z,\\,y⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that encloses the finite region between the paraboloid $z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ and the hemisphere $z=\\sqrt{5-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}.$ Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=\\sqrt{5-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper hemisphere ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{5-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{5-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{5-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{5-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=0,\\,Q=z=\\sqrt{5-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}},\\,R=y.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂h}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂h}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-0\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{5-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}})-\\sqrt{5-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{5-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}})+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(0+y+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower paraboloid ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=0,\\,Q=z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2},\\,R=y.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-0\\cdot (2x)-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\cdot (2y)+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(0-2y{x}^{2}-2{y}^{3}+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2y{x}^{2}+2{y}^{3}-y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2y\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}2y{x}^{2}+2{y}^{3}-y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2y{x}^{2}+2{y}^{3}+y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281386 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15501fc5-9f05-535a-afa5-9a19654f94b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{5}{2},\\,y\\le -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278781} {:answer/id #uuid "2ccf5191-8634-5b90-a807-77a0bbfed33b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le 0,\\,y\\ge 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278987} {:answer/id #uuid "2d91f8fb-c06a-5a6e-99ba-a8c38d385d08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le 5,\\,y\\ge 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278993} {:answer/id #uuid "3cd9f045-3487-5be5-adc3-3d5b3edc327f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge 4,\\,y\\le -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279127} {:answer/id #uuid "eeef9da7-ffa6-5021-aee7-5cc4d1113afc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le \\frac{25}{4},\\,y\\ge 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280684}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eeef9da7-ffa6-5021-aee7-5cc4d1113afc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le \\frac{25}{4},\\,y\\ge 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280684} :answer-field/id #uuid "43190c17-eebf-5453-8891-2414e96f88c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280947}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2dc3a2b7-620d-5940-a1aa-2a26502f43cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-16200" :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=5t-{t}^{2},\\,\\,\\,y={t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\ndetermine the ranges of $x$ and $y.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ we can start by completing the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =5t-{t}^{2} \\\\  & =-({t}^{2}-5t) \\\\  & =-{(t-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =-{(t-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{25}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${(t-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}\\ge 0,$ we can conclude that $x\\le \\frac{25}{4}.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ notice that ${t}^{2}\\ge 0$ for all $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Consequently, $y\\ge 0.$\n\nTherefore, the ranges are $x\\le \\frac{25}{4}$ and $y\\ge 0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281387 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d1ee7ca-524d-5e07-9932-963dc0249b1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278714} {:answer/id #uuid "1a6b093a-1f6c-5127-92d0-0c1e3b334c4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278832} {:answer/id #uuid "2d76e47b-1b3c-5896-81a0-30ab7fc0fc94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278992} {:answer/id #uuid "80c8bc1e-b087-5c2b-ac8d-b804e8b61eb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279749} {:answer/id #uuid "cbd94ac3-033c-5b2f-99d8-292b21591d33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280380}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a6b093a-1f6c-5127-92d0-0c1e3b334c4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278832} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c4b1df2-bfbd-5b54-9f98-003790a03fb6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280882}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0d7b3552-b54a-5c05-93a2-3660e2678f53" :question/math-academy-id "q-162147" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨x,\\,z,\\,y⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=\\frac{1}{2}x-y-1$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 3.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the upward-pointing unit normal vector $n.$\n\n*Hint: You're given that the fundamental vector product of $S$ with respect to its canonical parametrization is $||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||=\\frac{3}{2}.$*" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z=\\frac{1}{2}x-y-1$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot \\underbrace{⟨-\\frac{1}{2},\\,1,\\,1⟩}_{\\overset{~}{n}}=-1,\\,\\text{or}\\,⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot \\underbrace{⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\,-1,\\,-1⟩}_{-\\overset{~}{n}}=1.\n$$\n\nSince we require an upward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is positive. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{n}=⟨-\\frac{1}{2},\\,1,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨-\\frac{1}{2},\\,1,\\,1⟩}{\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}⟨-\\frac{1}{2},\\,1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨-1,\\,2,\\,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=\\frac{1}{2}x-y-1$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨x,\\,z,\\,y⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}⟨-1,\\,2,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨x,\\,\\frac{1}{2}x-y-1,\\,y⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,2,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(-x+(x-2y-2)+2y) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}(-\\frac{2}{3})\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-\\frac{2}{3})\\cdot ||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-\\frac{2}{3})\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-\\frac{\\cancel{2}}{\\cancel{3}})\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{3}}{\\cancel{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-1\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281432 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21f536c1-271d-5b14-86ec-ab60c8c26c0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278899} {:answer/id #uuid "4209236d-9593-5b62-af48-159ac843b640" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279180} {:answer/id #uuid "46f2ab15-a5f0-5d18-82c3-edd55066cc1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279224} {:answer/id #uuid "a9c77178-f0da-5abe-9f83-86ad5f68ac83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280099} {:answer/id #uuid "b01a657f-e9bb-5c61-ac82-f073f17fd78b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280151}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46f2ab15-a5f0-5d18-82c3-edd55066cc1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279224} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf2966ba-8339-5442-b6ce-44c586c5356e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281195}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7f222b3a-eb4c-598d-98b0-57764691bb32" :question/math-academy-id "q-16228" :question/problem "What is the general solution to the equation  $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}?$  Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $2$nd quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\n![](images/bf/bf1efb64724fb0dfbf60cc14905e63ba867caa0bf0edd888b35fb554af97c856.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$  lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution for the given equation is\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi .\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281440 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0073c988-ab13-5d35-82dc-f86a2f72a0de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278588} {:answer/id #uuid "268b4c33-c226-5773-b9b1-46201fc827d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278936} {:answer/id #uuid "8bc11660-2038-5011-af7b-4ccf1db2e5e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\pi -1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279836} {:answer/id #uuid "c41bff5d-e1eb-5b17-a3c4-b0bc834579fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280318} {:answer/id #uuid "c71ff19f-ae04-593f-8810-e601bcf14f11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280338}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0073c988-ab13-5d35-82dc-f86a2f72a0de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278588} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd81652e-0bcb-5dc4-b49d-1d1e352b840b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281282}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "55c78036-de48-5eea-9253-f540a61a0813" :question/math-academy-id "q-162330" :question/problem "Evaluate\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{arccos}⁡y}{\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y.$\n\n*Hint: First change the order of integration to $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z.$*" :question/worked-solution "We wish to find the limits of integration such that our integral can be expressed as \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(z)}^{{v}_{2}(z)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(y,z)}^{{u}_{2}(y,z)}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the domain of integration $R$ can be written as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le \\operatorname{arccos}⁡y,\\,\\,0\\le x\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\nSince all repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $x,$ the $x$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(z)}^{{v}_{2}(z)}{\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $yz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](images/c5/c5f4ec9b9b12411f3057bc074a14a1f541c768c30d3a9746d7a0484b0491ad2e.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is type I region in the $yz$-plane:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,0\\le z\\le \\operatorname{arccos}⁡y\\}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y$ to $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z,$ we write $D$ as a type II region.\n\nNotice that the left boundary of the region $D$ is\n\n$$\ny=0\n$$\n\nand the right boundary of the region $D,$ written in the form $y=y(z),$ is\n\n$$\nz=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡y\\,⟹\\,y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡z.\n$$\n\nThen, the type II representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/07/077fc8133673cd57aba570cca9a6e0f5f25aa742720948c46db2b9e1d05add69.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{arccos}⁡y}{\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡z}{\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡z}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡z}y\\,[x]_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡z}2y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡z}2y\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}[{y}^{2}]_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡z}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡z\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{2}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2z)\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{[z+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2z]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281441 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "156a47fd-2c85-5208-95ad-5d50ddbcf677" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi -4}{48}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278783} {:answer/id #uuid "57ad6671-46bc-5a4d-9af6-b9f89965cadb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi +1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279397} {:answer/id #uuid "8e121028-a9bf-5ba0-a4cb-3fb4471c6815" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\pi -3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279856} {:answer/id #uuid "b259ada9-1a8f-5265-8931-8e97c4a5a526" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4(2\\pi +1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280167} {:answer/id #uuid "e55e7203-a0f9-5ce6-ac49-337eb9365639" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280617}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "156a47fd-2c85-5208-95ad-5d50ddbcf677" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi -4}{48}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278783} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0dcb97f-3379-54fe-a421-8db4631dab43" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281260}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "63019a17-1ba4-5f61-b529-aa74e153abd8" :question/math-academy-id "q-162331" :question/problem "Evaluate\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡z)/2}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{0}^{2yz}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{z})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z.$\n\n*Hint: First change the order of integration to $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y.$*" :question/worked-solution "We wish to find the limits of integration such that our integral can be expressed as \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(y)}^{{v}_{2}(y)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(y,z)}^{{u}_{2}(y,z)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{z})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the domain of integration $R$ can be written as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡z\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,\\,0\\le x\\le 2yz}\n$$\n\nSince all repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $x,$ the $x$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(y)}^{{v}_{2}(y)}{\\int }_{0}^{2yz}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{z})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $yz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](images/d3/d3865f790df8c2a228f08c290a113954687ade63a275a754c4cb3199c234d5ec.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is type II region in the $yz$-plane:\n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡z\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z$ to $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y,$ we write $D$ as a type I region.\n\nNotice that the bottom boundary of the region $D$ is\n\n$$\nz=0\n$$\n\nand the top boundary of the region $D,$ written in the form $z=z(y),$ is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡z\\,⟹\\,z=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y.\n$$\n\nThen, the type I representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,0\\le z\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡2y},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/08/085b2ce5a9ecd2632f70f287bbef5e9f04566871fc8adf7ba10076e2e875ca2e.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡z)/2}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{0}^{2yz}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{z})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y}{\\int }_{0}^{2yz}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{z})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y}[{\\int }_{0}^{2yz}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{z})\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y}{[-z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{z})]}_{x=0}^{x=2yz}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y}(-z\\operatorname{cos}⁡2y+z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y}z(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2y)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}[(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2y){\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y}z\\,\\text{d}z]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2y){[\\frac{1}{2}{z}^{2}]}_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2y){sin}^{2}⁡2y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{sin}^{2}⁡2y\\,\\text{d}y-\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2y\\,{sin}^{2}⁡2y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡4y)\\,\\text{d}y-\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2y\\,{sin}^{2}⁡2y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{[y-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4y]}_{0}^{\\pi /4}-\\frac{1}{12}[{sin}^{3}⁡2y]_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}-0)-\\frac{1}{12}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi -4}{48}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281443 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48e2a033-443c-584c-a15f-eea6d9dae46d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279242} {:answer/id #uuid "642f63b8-b430-5120-8afa-60337721d5a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi e+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279503} {:answer/id #uuid "f1d73ed0-76b2-520e-9de6-dc0f038550ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi e}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280698} {:answer/id #uuid "f5cf9286-3e28-5594-96ef-d23587fa7550" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi (e-2)}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280744} {:answer/id #uuid "f899ec44-2bfd-5d46-ac07-d2e8f6a0ae6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280775}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f5cf9286-3e28-5594-96ef-d23587fa7550" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi (e-2)}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280744} :answer-field/id #uuid "06018cfb-ebc1-54fb-ba81-4655903daaf9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280840}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "11b88039-dcd3-5fe3-9ad3-e8f58ab164bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-162334" :question/problem "Evaluate\n${\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡z}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}y}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z.$\n\n*Hint: First change the order of integration to $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y.$*" :question/worked-solution "We wish to find the limits of integration such that our integral can be expressed as \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(y)}^{{v}_{2}(y)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(y,z)}^{{u}_{2}(y,z)}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the domain of integration $R$ can be written as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le z\\le e,\\,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡z\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le x\\le \\sqrt{3}y\\}\n$$\n\nSince all repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $x,$ the $x$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(y)}^{{v}_{2}(y)}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}y}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $yz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](images/14/14c67512e143995cbfcfcf9e80c8d0975bbbc42368c97aa29956bd2d3f3454a8.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is type II region in the $yz$-plane:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le z\\le e,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡z\\le y\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z$ to $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y,$ we write $D$ as a type I region.\n\nNotice that the bottom boundary of the region $D$ is\n\n$$\nz=1\n$$\n\nand the top boundary of the region $D,$ written in the form $z=z(y),$ is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{ln}⁡z\\,⟹\\,z={e}^{y}.\n$$\n\nThen, the type I representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,1\\le z\\le {e}^{y}\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/d1/d18470b45d21b4734187c1f639784a4e75924ed212e3fa4c7a214d19eaeb6022.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡z}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}y}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{y}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}y}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{y}}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}y}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{y}}{[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{y})]}_{x=0}^{x=\\sqrt{3}y}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{y}}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{y}}\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\frac{\\pi }{3}{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{y}}\\text{d}z]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{\\pi }{3}[z]_{1}^{{e}^{y}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{\\pi }{3}({e}^{y}-1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}[{e}^{y}-y]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}(e-1-1+0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi (e-2)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281447 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d65d348-380b-5f1a-8d6d-4fe1b2db6b74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449988} {:answer/id #uuid "59c93832-fe98-545b-859c-ae8eb173bd4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449989}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d65d348-380b-5f1a-8d6d-4fe1b2db6b74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449988} :answer-field/id #uuid "5dd1bf69-489b-5265-9fa9-4746d265dca2" :answer-field/key "${w}^{4}+1=1$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450948} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc47b583-9334-5b64-b2a1-d1741b28cf8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449990} {:answer/id #uuid "fe392d18-15aa-5a5b-9916-5395ad1e6647" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449991}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fe392d18-15aa-5a5b-9916-5395ad1e6647" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449991} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0d3092d-28e8-5f0f-8af6-41deece82164" :answer-field/key "${(\\frac{z}{w})}^{4}=1$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450949} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c096ebcf-8397-5faf-9dd6-c2509fd106b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449992} {:answer/id #uuid "655d1d09-5adb-5ec9-b4bd-060d64f54341" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449993}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "655d1d09-5adb-5ec9-b4bd-060d64f54341" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449993} :answer-field/id #uuid "bddffa24-0649-52c9-a5aa-e2628f8f7a10" :answer-field/key "$\\frac{z}{w}$ is a $4$th root of unity" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450950}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "46fbce01-7db5-5630-8364-44d4a151bbf9" :question/math-academy-id "q-162346" :question/problem "Let $z$ and $w$ be $4$th roots of unity. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement ${w}^{4}+1=1$ is false. If $z$ and $w$ are $4$th roots of unity, then (by definition) we have that \n\n$$\n{z}^{4}={w}^{4}=1.\n$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\n{w}^{4}+1=1+1=2\\ne 1.\n$$\n\n- Statement ${(\\frac{z}{w})}^{4}=1$ is true. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{z}{w})}^{4} & =\\frac{{z}^{4}}{{w}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$\\frac{z}{w}$ is a $4$th root of unity” is true. From statement ${(\\frac{z}{w})}^{4}=1$, we have that ${(\\frac{z}{w})}^{4}=1,$ which means that $\\frac{z}{w}$ is a $4$th root of unity. \n\nIn general, the quotient of $n$th roots of unity is an $n$th root of unity.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${(\\frac{z}{w})}^{4}=1$; “$\\frac{z}{w}$ is a $4$th root of unity” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281462 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0933d0f3-e5d9-58cf-b64e-3945452219c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-{60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278681} {:answer/id #uuid "29af026e-02ed-55ef-a4e8-ed60bde5d84b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278959} {:answer/id #uuid "8d44e69f-0d29-58fb-859f-d376396ad842" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-{30}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279851} {:answer/id #uuid "8ef6a881-d17f-5d7d-a213-e0177ffc9a13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={30}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279864} {:answer/id #uuid "fcf2bb9e-3570-56f0-83c4-8ce1987665fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-{150}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280805}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d44e69f-0d29-58fb-859f-d376396ad842" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-{30}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279851} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0d7c23b-ad14-5c9f-89a5-da5f07d7dbc2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281259}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9c430e98-b261-5e82-9f7b-b768850b5575" :question/math-academy-id "q-16239" :question/problem "In degrees, find the general solution to the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.$  Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})=-{30}^{∘}\n$$\n\nThe tangent function has a period of ${180}^{∘}.$  Hence, the general solution for the given equation is: \n\n$$\nx=-{30}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281469 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43d7d11c-5e09-580d-9138-b2bcbdadc1b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279201} {:answer/id #uuid "8f1f84b4-0821-51d6-89b0-fead6e75723e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279865} {:answer/id #uuid "abca34d9-3dbe-5d7a-8d86-11d35949a569" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280113} {:answer/id #uuid "bfe657e7-03d8-5b7e-922e-2f4b3a890faa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280279} {:answer/id #uuid "f43f243d-8c70-54f6-9400-9bf0fe9a5dd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280729}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "abca34d9-3dbe-5d7a-8d86-11d35949a569" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280113} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b1d5e59-f911-53c6-a483-5c396f9856e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281088}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b29a950e-ff6a-53c6-9c95-7f20926ddb5b" :question/math-academy-id "q-162404" :question/problem "Find the radius of curvature for the curve $r(t)=({t}^{2}-1)\\,i+(1-t)\\,j$ at $t=\\frac{1}{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(t)$ of a plane curve $r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{|{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)|}{([{x}^{′}(t)]^{2}+[{y}^{′}(t)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nThe radius of curvature $R$ is defined as\n$R(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}.$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)={t}^{2}-1,\\,y(t)=1-t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =2t,\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =-1 \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =2,\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{|2t\\cdot 0-(-1)\\cdot 2|}{{((2t)^{2}+(-1)^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{(4{t}^{2}+1)}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=\\frac{1}{2}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(\\frac{1}{2}) & =\\frac{2}{{(4{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+1)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{(1+1)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the radius of curvature at $t=\\frac{1}{2}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(\\frac{1}{2}) & =\\frac{1}{κ(\\frac{1}{2})}=\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281476 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "117c1fa2-7141-5aa8-b40d-148f67d446b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{11\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278754} {:answer/id #uuid "4d872866-2f0f-5e20-b48a-dbdac66ab9c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{11\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279293} {:answer/id #uuid "8a1964cc-2731-52b7-8343-7ede77555f4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279825} {:answer/id #uuid "a40bab4e-db4b-5a69-8ba1-cdba9b1a316d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280048} {:answer/id #uuid "f2c05b0e-ac3f-51eb-85f6-1b15984fbce2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280708}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a1964cc-2731-52b7-8343-7ede77555f4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279825} :answer-field/id #uuid "72091780-a1d8-5ddb-8fb4-4ae3b1b292df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281035}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8e7354de-3336-5f3b-9f0e-c5e5d8b1917d" :question/math-academy-id "q-16246" :question/problem "![](images/f6/f6e3ee6eed44a5ecb7e037e9cd8401a95b3e2a4640bd4454f60019f5bd9d1e67.png)\n\nThe graph shows a portion of the curve $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and the line $y=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Which of the following generates the $x$-values of all of their points of intersection? Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "At the intersection points, the $y$-values are the same for both graphs. This gives the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}.$ \n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $1$st quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the second quadrant, and it also has ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\n![](images/00/00d540b1f6412e4af2617452fbf0c58750d01e844d89d0d92cfacc0cb3e45e32.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$  lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution for the given equation is\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi .\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281477 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4166d235-26fd-55c2-ba75-a545a0c6714c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279175} {:answer/id #uuid "4c4f5558-c7b3-50d9-9d22-de8fab0fd956" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279275} {:answer/id #uuid "4d57d1fb-25b1-5c73-89a3-d36dba6a22dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279287} {:answer/id #uuid "6d4e5861-a325-58b9-b231-448c50cb7791" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279588} {:answer/id #uuid "ce9e25be-40ab-5eca-8a7c-df19ac612398" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280412}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c4f5558-c7b3-50d9-9d22-de8fab0fd956" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279275} :answer-field/id #uuid "14e1b3a4-df1f-517d-a217-9d6a9ba2488c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280867}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a7c93d46-b6af-5493-bffe-c7cad1a03b63" :question/math-academy-id "q-162476" :question/problem "Find the radius of the curvature for the curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)⟩$ at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(t)$ of a plane curve $r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{|{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)|}{([{x}^{′}(t)]^{2}+[{y}^{′}(t)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nThe radius of curvature $R$ is defined as\n$R(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}.$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,y(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & =-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{|2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))-(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))|}{{((2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))^{2}+(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-8{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)-8{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)|}{{(4{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)+4{sin}^{2}⁡(2t))}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the radius of curvature at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{1}{κ(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281481 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08d7da73-46d6-5316-8e9d-cfef40186113" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278676} {:answer/id #uuid "5a47a481-58a0-59c7-9e07-3db8c5ebb32b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279422} {:answer/id #uuid "89f3c7b5-6c51-542b-8a68-e751c820d38f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{\\pi }^{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279821} {:answer/id #uuid "d03dd525-9b98-5a94-a0e3-be23821761c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280424} {:answer/id #uuid "d812aee3-9933-50fd-941f-70d98fa1fbf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280501}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a47a481-58a0-59c7-9e07-3db8c5ebb32b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279422} :answer-field/id #uuid "8bde6804-bc0f-5e7e-9d81-a93c536afab2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281090}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7f366230-b238-5167-9be4-c77ac1ad35fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-162500" :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{{\\pi }^{2}/4}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{x}}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{z}{y}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{y})\\,dy\\,dx\\,dz=$\n\n*Hint: Change the order of integration to $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z.$*" :question/worked-solution "We cannot integrate the given integral because we cannot find a simple antiderivative of the integrand. So, we change the order of integration.\n\nWe wish to find the limits of integration ${u}_{1}(y,z),$ ${u}_{2}(y,z),$ ${v}_{1}(z),$ ${v}_{2}(z),$ $a,$ and $b$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{{\\pi }^{2}/4}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{x}}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{z}{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{y})\\,dy\\,dx\\,dz={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(z)}^{{v}_{2}(z)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(y,z)}^{{u}_{2}(y,z)}\\frac{z}{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{y})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the region of integration $R$ can be written as a type III region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4},\\,\\,\\sqrt{x}\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch the region. Since it is a type III region, we first sketch the projection onto the $xz$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nE={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4},\\,\\,0\\le z\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/29/29c722bce3c23c2af628f18dfaf16695f9ec5beac68b0d16ce93d6a993d6fb8c.png)\n\nThen, the region $R$ is shown in the diagram below, where $\\sqrt{x}\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\n![](images/cd/cdc8fb2c7f6ef9a28b01094ef3bf18089297a6ef9a431c5152311e5e92cd2557.png)\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z$ to $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z,$ we write $R$ as a type II region and its projection $D$ onto the $yz$-plane as a type II *plane* region.\n\nTo visualize this, imagine firing an arrow through the region parallel to the $x$-axis and in the direction of increasing $x,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/d8/d805df26c5a471323d855194b41e052142e711e7f7f9ddf910d792ae74cbc2c6.png)\n\nThe arrow enters the region through the back boundary and leaves through the front boundary:\n\n- The back boundary of the region is\n\n$$\nx=0.\n$$\n\n- The front boundary of the region, written in the form $x=x(y,z),$ is\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{x}\\,⟹\\,x={y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the type II representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le x\\le {y}^{2}},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/4d/4d40e420abeec633cd145dca33071d6459e4b4009e4c6cfc871d6c6150be51e4.png)\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ as a type II plane region in the $yz$-plane with the left curve given by $y=\\sqrt{0}=0.$\n\n![](images/81/81782a6c847e5369dfed7524619de354ff7676a856685fb4340d09cf61dca339.png)\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\,0\\le z\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{{\\pi }^{2}/4}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{x}}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{z}{y}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{y})\\,dy\\,dx\\,dz & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}\\frac{z}{y}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{y})\\,dx\\,dy\\,dz \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{z}{y}[{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{y})\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{z}{y}{[y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{y})]}_{x=0}^{x={y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{z}{y}(y\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-0)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}z\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}z\\,\\text{d}z]\\cdot [{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y] \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}{z}^{2}]}_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\cdot [-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}-0)\\cdot (-0+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281489 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23c7a7e9-da7d-5c85-866c-b2fe189ab9d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4{ln}^{2}⁡2}{(4+{ln}^{2}⁡2)^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278912} {:answer/id #uuid "3af6f758-92f1-5104-b867-e764a1444773" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{ln}^{2}⁡2}{(2+{ln}^{2}⁡2)^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279116} {:answer/id #uuid "96ae20c4-d2ba-5df1-9ef7-ee8dda62071d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4{ln}^{2}⁡2}{(1+{ln}^{2}⁡2)^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279925} {:answer/id #uuid "d5735b14-23fc-5b32-8069-3b4c3988f129" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2{ln}^{2}⁡2}{(1+{ln}^{2}⁡2)^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280475} {:answer/id #uuid "ddd0405b-e35c-5d85-8bc3-a5a6eedb7d73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8{ln}^{2}⁡2}{(4+{ln}^{2}⁡2)^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280547}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23c7a7e9-da7d-5c85-866c-b2fe189ab9d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4{ln}^{2}⁡2}{(4+{ln}^{2}⁡2)^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278912} :answer-field/id #uuid "b447dd2e-4a24-53d5-85f0-852828cc0edf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281153}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d2059e5f-93a8-5b5f-ab4e-bc10c8876989" :question/math-academy-id "q-162534" :question/problem "Find the signed curvature of the curve $y={2}^{x}$ at $x=-1.$" :question/worked-solution "The signed curvature $k(x)$ of a plane curve $y=y(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nk(x)=\\frac{{y}^{″}(x)}{(1+[{y}^{′}(x)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${y}^{′}(x)$ and ${y}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)={2}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,{y}^{″}(x)={2}^{x}{ln}^{2}⁡2\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the signed curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(x) & =\\frac{{2}^{x}{ln}^{2}⁡2}{{[1+{({2}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)}^{2}]}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{x}{ln}^{2}⁡2}{{(1+{2}^{2x}{ln}^{2}⁡2)}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $x=-1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(-1) & =\\frac{{2}^{-1}{ln}^{2}⁡2}{{(1+{2}^{2(-1)}{ln}^{2}⁡2)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡2}{2})}{{(\\frac{4+{ln}^{2}⁡2}{4})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{ln}^{2}⁡2}{(4+{ln}^{2}⁡2)^{3/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281495 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e9f6b3b-013a-5d23-ba87-538ff7611718" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278878} {:answer/id #uuid "7214f6ce-83f0-5443-84fc-20acb98afa84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "216\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279629} {:answer/id #uuid "7732cf67-03f6-59f5-9b25-da6ee7ccf96a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "243\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279655} {:answer/id #uuid "a3368fe0-436e-54bb-8a95-53b442357715" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "81\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280041} {:answer/id #uuid "b9becb59-4c7c-59d1-81e0-eb9920f32f78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "128\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280226}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7214f6ce-83f0-5443-84fc-20acb98afa84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "216\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279629} :answer-field/id #uuid "a60449cf-9d8b-5e61-bc2d-a468d2e83811" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281122}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6aee075a-fd15-5236-b83e-02176157c22a" :question/math-academy-id "q-162547" :question/problem "![](images/de/dedd58b5eeeba8b2874c740543132026f95157597dd12ad31fbaa476a3d77c2e.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨0,{y}^{2}(6-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}),\\,0⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of a right circular cylinder of height $4$ whose base is a circle of radius $3$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $y$-component of $F=⟨0,{y}^{2}(6-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}),\\,0⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral side of $S$ is parallel to the $y$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the left and right sides. \n\n![](images/9f/9ffcdd701a8ac6b12cd62773e1cabfd2ce95f9a809e18d087c4bf4f4a22eadb5.png)\n\nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the right surface. On this surface, we have $y=4$ for ${x}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 9.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $y=4$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,{y}^{2}(6-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}),\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,({4}^{2})(6-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}),\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,16(6-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}),\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =16(6-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}16(6-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2})\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =16{∬}_{D}(6-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying a change of variables $(x,z)\\to (r,\\theta ),\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16{∬}_{D}(6-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & =16{∬}_{D}(6-{r}^{2})\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =16{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}r(6-{r}^{2})\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =16{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}(6r-{r}^{3})\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =16{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{3}(6r-{r}^{3})\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =16\\cdot 2\\pi \\cdot {[3{r}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =32\\pi (27-\\frac{81}{4}) \\\\  & =32\\pi (\\frac{27}{4}) \\\\  & =216\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the left surface. On this surface, we have $y=0$ for ${x}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 9.$ Therefore, for everywhere on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nF(x,0,z)=⟨0,\\,{0}^{2}(6-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}),\\,0⟩=⟨0,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =216\\pi +0 \\\\  & =216\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281537 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f38cf8b-627c-54b8-abf8-e8bbecd33d62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279311} {:answer/id #uuid "99a946f3-e605-5c59-bc1d-615ab79b5af5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{38}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279949} {:answer/id #uuid "c10820a2-4268-59f9-9e94-a86ff060af0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280287} {:answer/id #uuid "d6b0198b-bfd9-512d-b8a3-209536eb8e7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280488} {:answer/id #uuid "fa645086-70dc-5c8b-b6a7-90c41a6fe771" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{41}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280790}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "99a946f3-e605-5c59-bc1d-615ab79b5af5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{38}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279949} :answer-field/id #uuid "70167a37-adf4-53db-a118-8674cdc73349" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281030}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c88bf56e-e321-5643-94de-ec7d4990727f" :question/math-academy-id "q-162766" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y,z)=2x+y+5z$ on the ellipsoid $9{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+9{z}^{2}=38.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the maximum value of $f$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y,z)=9{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+9{z}^{2}=38.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=38\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2,1,5⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨18x,2y,18z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2 &=18\\lambda x \\\\ 1 &=2\\lambda y \\\\ 5 &=18\\lambda z\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=\\frac{1}{9\\lambda } \\\\ y &=\\frac{1}{2\\lambda } \\\\ z &=\\frac{5}{18\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x,\\,y$ and $z$ into the condition $g(x,y,z)=38$ and solve for $\\lambda ,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+9{z}^{2} & =38 \\\\ 9(\\frac{1}{81{\\lambda }^{2}})+\\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+9(\\frac{25}{324{\\lambda }^{2}}) & =38 \\\\ \\frac{1}{9{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{25}{36{\\lambda }^{2}} & =38 \\\\ \\frac{19}{18{\\lambda }^{2}} & =38 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{36} \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $x,\\,y$ and $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx=\\pm \\frac{2}{3},\\,y=\\pm 3,\\,z=\\pm \\frac{5}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points \n\n$$\n(\\frac{2}{3},3,\\frac{5}{3}),\\,(-\\frac{2}{3},-3,-\\frac{5}{3}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,(-\\frac{2}{3},-3,-\\frac{5}{3})\\,$ | $\\,(\\frac{2}{3},3,\\frac{5}{3})\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,-\\frac{38}{3}\\,$ | $\\,\\frac{38}{3}\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(\\frac{2}{3},3,\\frac{5}{3}),$ and its maximum value is $\\frac{38}{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281538 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05dc044b-24e6-5b1f-8787-d56ca91a9176" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278646} {:answer/id #uuid "7b0ba600-5352-5861-a7a7-22e72871086b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{21}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279690} {:answer/id #uuid "7e357422-bf28-5f1c-8a6c-e23d12be25da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279725} {:answer/id #uuid "b410e16c-dfd0-5424-90b9-d8368e817d9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{14}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280177} {:answer/id #uuid "f5f2419e-a59e-59f5-8bb8-6f6008d18dc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280745}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b0ba600-5352-5861-a7a7-22e72871086b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{21}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279690} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd67f28a-cbb2-587d-9c14-b9a7a306bc81" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281192}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "15a21e57-2278-5487-9586-a1ff33b69b24" :question/math-academy-id "q-162767" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y,z)=x+y+4z$ on the ellipsoid $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2}=21.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the minimum value of $f$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y,z)=4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2}=21.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=21\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,1,4⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨8x,2y,8z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1 &=8\\lambda x \\\\ 1 &=2\\lambda y \\\\ 4 &=8\\lambda z\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=\\frac{1}{8\\lambda } \\\\ y &=\\frac{1}{2\\lambda } \\\\ z &=\\frac{1}{2\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x,\\,y$ and $z$ into the condition $g(x,y,z)=21$ and solve for $\\lambda ,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2} & =21 \\\\ 4(\\frac{1}{64{\\lambda }^{2}})+\\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+4(\\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}) & =21 \\\\ \\frac{1}{16{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{\\lambda }^{2}} & =21 \\\\ \\frac{21}{16{\\lambda }^{2}} & =21 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $x,\\,y$ and $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx=\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,y=\\pm 2,\\,z=\\pm 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points \n\n$$\n(\\frac{1}{2},2,2),\\,(-\\frac{1}{2},-2,-2).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,(-\\frac{1}{2},-2,-2)\\,$ | $\\,(\\frac{1}{2},2,2)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,-\\frac{21}{2}\\,$ | $\\,\\frac{21}{2}\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(-\\frac{1}{2},-2,-2),$ and its minimum value is $-\\frac{21}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281548 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1715e06f-e211-5b5b-b085-4dce3fe10cc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278797} {:answer/id #uuid "4ae4dbb2-6216-51cc-9650-90dd9779ab6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279260} {:answer/id #uuid "b44db878-4ead-5dad-a363-920768dc38d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280179} {:answer/id #uuid "c1fb5f2f-a80f-5006-a9c4-be4750a711f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280294} {:answer/id #uuid "e19f9127-76af-507a-8b89-9b102194e39f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280586}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b44db878-4ead-5dad-a363-920768dc38d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280179} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9a1c281-1dcb-592f-8bb1-786e3c4e7bdc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281160}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b349e078-f370-57a4-8287-923743beec39" :question/math-academy-id "q-162790" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)=x-{y}^{2}$ given that ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,-2y⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the above equation has no solutions, we conclude that the function does not have critical values in the region's interior.\n\nNext, we need to find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=4\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,-2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1 &=2\\lambda x \\\\ -2y &=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda x &=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ y(1+\\lambda ) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $y=0$ in the second equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=4$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{0}^{2} & =4\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(\\pm 2,0).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =-1$ in the second equation of the system, the first equation of the system gives \n\n$$\n-x=\\frac{1}{2}\\,⇒\\,x=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\n \nThen, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=4$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(-\\frac{1}{2},\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2}).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(\\pm 2,0),\\,(-\\frac{1}{2},\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2}).\n$$\n\n \n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(2,0)$ | $(-2,0)$ | $(-\\frac{1}{2},\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $2$ | $-2$ | $-\\frac{17}{4}$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(2,0),$ and its maximum value is $2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281549 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f99e9c7-cb36-5228-9c41-28e4961f2d97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279009} {:answer/id #uuid "54d4e3a5-d812-5fba-b7ce-960cac114d20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279363} {:answer/id #uuid "9c9dc478-f346-5793-b1e8-9c5026604f5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279985} {:answer/id #uuid "9d2eb8b5-a992-5816-af7a-c95dadf4579b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279989} {:answer/id #uuid "f33bbf3a-c060-52f1-a2ed-041e9b5e1854" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280716}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f99e9c7-cb36-5228-9c41-28e4961f2d97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279009} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4166733-a580-5eab-a6a6-bfacef9ce080" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281231}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7651aa63-b4a3-56d9-b622-a30f9579f57e" :question/math-academy-id "q-162791" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)=-4x+y+4\\,$ on the parabola $8x-{y}^{2}=1.$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that the global maximum of $f(x,y)$ exists on the parabola.*" :question/worked-solution "We must find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=8x-{y}^{2}=1.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=1\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨-4,1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨8,-2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-4 &=8\\lambda \\\\ 1 &=-2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda &=-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ y &=-\\frac{1}{2\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now use $\\lambda $ to find $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{2(-\\frac{1}{2})}=1.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expression for $y$ into the condition $g(x,y)=1$ and solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8x-{y}^{2}=1 \\\\ 8x-1 & =1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has a possible extreme value at the point\n\n$$\n(\\frac{1}{4},1).\n$$\n\nSince the given parabola opens to the right, there isn't a minimum value of $f$ on the parabola. Thus, the extreme value is the maximum of $f$ on the parabola. We evaluate $f$ at this point:\n\n| $\\,(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,(\\frac{1}{4},1)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,4\\,$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(\\frac{1}{4},1),$ and its maximum value is $4.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281561 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "023f3c24-7fe0-5840-a5d6-809d8e39927d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278602} {:answer/id #uuid "6b891af7-6f5f-5eaf-950c-57035bf987c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279568} {:answer/id #uuid "bbb07a70-2283-5402-854b-2e586af0ddbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{64}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280242} {:answer/id #uuid "d6f1470b-6007-5e6b-b19f-f373d9e47ce3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280489} {:answer/id #uuid "f75a797c-d0fa-553b-9ecc-505014a0d515" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280760}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6f1470b-6007-5e6b-b19f-f373d9e47ce3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280489} :answer-field/id #uuid "51bae934-f9e0-5c93-a239-1690cff765fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280964}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "24e60831-e83f-5ffa-a929-6a3941104ab1" :question/math-academy-id "q-162981" :question/problem "Consider a thin rod along the $x$-axis over the interval $0\\le x\\le 4$ whose mass density function is $\\lambda (x)=\\sqrt{x}.$ Find the $x$-coordinate of the center of mass of the rod." :question/worked-solution "The center of mass of a rod placed along the $x$-axis over the interval $a\\le x\\le b$ whose mass density function is $\\lambda (x)$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $m={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is the mass of the rod,\n\n- ${M}_{O}={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\,x\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is the moment of the rod about the origin $O.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x)=\\sqrt{x},\\,a=0,\\,b=4.\n$$\n\nSo, the mass of the rod is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{x}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3/2}]}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {4}^{3/2}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand the moment of the rod about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}x\\cdot \\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{x}^{3/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{2}{5}{x}^{5/2}]}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {4}^{5/2}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m}=\\frac{(\\frac{64}{5})}{(\\frac{16}{3})}=\\frac{12}{5}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281565 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7ab53738-993e-59ba-a1e8-6bb955f46de0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279684} {:answer/id #uuid "a6831946-5019-5b85-9063-1b4dab620eda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280066} {:answer/id #uuid "b280e2f2-55be-5bf6-95bb-797c1636d7c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280169} {:answer/id #uuid "c23fb48f-217d-54f4-a91c-cc54d14ec0d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280299} {:answer/id #uuid "d18bc7ea-ff7c-57a0-a463-a74cf375f0fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280441}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b280e2f2-55be-5bf6-95bb-797c1636d7c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280169} :answer-field/id #uuid "4410fe5e-400a-5a84-991b-82e4ac1cc320" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280949}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b911aeca-0e09-5dc0-b2cb-6f0f1115cce0" :question/math-academy-id "q-163017" :question/problem "Consider a thin rod placed along the $x$-axis over the interval $0\\le x\\le 1$ whose mass density function is $\\lambda (x)=x-{x}^{3}.$ Given that the rod's mass is $\\frac{1}{4}$ units, what is the $x$-coordinate of its center of mass?" :question/worked-solution "The center of mass, denoted $\\overset{―}{x},$ of a rod placed along the $x$-axis over the interval $a\\le x\\le b$ whose mass density function is $\\lambda (x)$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $m={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is the mass of the rod,\n\n- ${M}_{O}={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\,x\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is the moment of the rod about the origin $O.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x)=x-{x}^{3},\\,a=0,\\,b=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the moment of the rod about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\cdot (x-{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}-{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{5}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m}=\\frac{(\\frac{2}{15})}{(\\frac{1}{4})}=\\frac{8}{15}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281566 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a71c1b3-91c2-5f52-845f-04435742cc02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278966} {:answer/id #uuid "829887b6-f8aa-5df6-8883-a0dedb9aa152" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279761} {:answer/id #uuid "ac514e81-2c67-5a54-adf6-8edfee4cc4c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280118} {:answer/id #uuid "d14a622b-c91d-516a-8818-4f3b6665541f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280439} {:answer/id #uuid "d87e9950-be36-5abb-9c69-4e617ff6b16b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280505}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "829887b6-f8aa-5df6-8883-a0dedb9aa152" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279761} :answer-field/id #uuid "723b4281-3410-59bf-b913-719f822ced96" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281036}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "48efa884-8150-5e6b-8d0a-1ebe0194039d" :question/math-academy-id "q-163019" :question/problem "![](images/88/887bc91e2565fa24630af4cb74b28723783e31f4769495f0a28a622d73d176de.png)\n\nThe thin rod above has the mass density function $\\lambda (x)=x+{x}^{2}.$ Given that the mass of the rod is $\\frac{5}{6}$ units, what is the $x$-coordinate of its center of mass?" :question/worked-solution "The center of mass of a rod placed along the $x$-axis over the interval $a\\le x\\le b$ whose mass density function is $\\lambda (x)$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $m={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is the mass of the rod,\n\n- ${M}_{O}={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\,x\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is the moment of the rod about the origin $O.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x)=x+{x}^{2},\\,a=0,\\,b=1.\n$$\n\nSo,  the moment of the rod about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\cdot (x+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}+{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m}=\\frac{(\\frac{7}{12})}{(\\frac{5}{6})}=\\frac{7}{10}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281567 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c78a1ef-5fb0-5540-89b2-4659460ab533" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279277} {:answer/id #uuid "4ed3829a-89a5-519d-80bf-3569b4e61c27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279308} {:answer/id #uuid "55157823-eaaf-53df-99da-d2f03b152f9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279366} {:answer/id #uuid "92c1b59c-089e-517f-88ba-5a375cc226ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279895} {:answer/id #uuid "cd9775fb-c043-54c2-9e71-eafa3d190c03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280404}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55157823-eaaf-53df-99da-d2f03b152f9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279366} :answer-field/id #uuid "45aaf83c-f68b-5099-ab34-db20ecc9dffc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280951}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "085351ec-f493-50f6-a73c-ef9589568633" :question/math-academy-id "q-163022" :question/problem "![](images/54/54e5dd5fdea01c02b968a6040068e190676841cb8d7b7fc05c561e02d522bbf1.png)\n\nThe thin rod above has the mass density function $\\lambda (x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}.$ Given that the mass of the rod is $\\frac{15}{32}$ units, what is the $x$-coordinate of its center of mass?" :question/worked-solution "The center of mass of a rod placed along the $x$-axis over the interval $a\\le x\\le b$ whose mass density function is $\\lambda (x)$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $m={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is the mass of the rod,\n\n- ${M}_{O}={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\,x\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is the moment of the rod about the origin $O.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}},\\,a=1,\\,b=4.\n$$\n\nSo,  the moment of the rod about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}x\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{x}]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m}=\\frac{(\\frac{3}{4})}{(\\frac{15}{32})}=\\frac{8}{5}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281570 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0072a6bc-bcf0-53b9-9a48-720f5054cc41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278587} {:answer/id #uuid "0524c95c-8812-5349-9386-45c8f675ab97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278637} {:answer/id #uuid "3972def6-c7d8-515a-87e9-0534bfbd5657" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279096} {:answer/id #uuid "83e4f599-c3c2-5dcc-b944-f86afba9dcd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279775} {:answer/id #uuid "e104cfe2-82e9-570e-8703-bd68c8b20aeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280584}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0072a6bc-bcf0-53b9-9a48-720f5054cc41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278587} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb6d0f0d-92a3-5001-9ef4-52626e87f169" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281164}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "90019212-5ef4-5914-92e3-b64c1a7668c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-163026" :question/problem "Consider a thin rod along the $x$-axis over the interval $0\\le x\\le 2$ whose mass density function is $\\lambda (x)=8-{x}^{3}.$ Find the $x$-coordinate of the center of mass of the rod." :question/worked-solution "The center of mass of a rod placed along the $x$-axis over the interval $a\\le x\\le b$ whose mass density function is $\\lambda (x)$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $m={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is the mass of the rod,\n\n- ${M}_{O}={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\,x\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is the moment of the rod about the origin $O.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x)=8-{x}^{3},\\,a=0,\\,b=2.\n$$\n\nSo, the mass of the rod is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}8-{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[8x-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =8\\cdot 2-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 16 \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand the moment of the rod about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\cdot (8-{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}8x-{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[4{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{5}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 4-\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 32 \\\\  & =\\frac{48}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m}=\\frac{(\\frac{48}{5})}{12}=\\frac{4}{5}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281580 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03690a0f-3b07-589a-b8c3-731cc1273779" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278613} {:answer/id #uuid "0ed7d2f6-5312-5ac6-b68d-2b7c1f0e9841" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278725} {:answer/id #uuid "8cf7e31b-d1f9-561d-a34a-34da9aa2f7a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279847} {:answer/id #uuid "d06e4199-8805-5767-8d40-3612a6a9089d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280425} {:answer/id #uuid "d3c4932c-ce50-5a46-9b6a-43e4ca548386" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280457}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d06e4199-8805-5767-8d40-3612a6a9089d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280425} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc149c5c-5921-5c0e-b29e-964a4a8b2a6e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281168}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6b4008ba-06f1-5e76-bd20-b28bf061598c" :question/math-academy-id "q-163046" :question/problem "For the transformation $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{x}{y},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\ncalculate ${f}^{′}$ at $a=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose a transformation $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{f}_{1}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{m}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${f}_{1},{f}_{2},…,{f}_{m}$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $f$ is differentiable at $x,$ then the total derivative of $f$ at $x,$ denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x}{y})=\\frac{1}{y}, & \\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{x}{y})=-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{y} & -\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nand ${f}^{′}$ at $a=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ is\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{(1)} & -\\frac{(-2)}{(1)^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281588 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18f5a574-0cca-57fa-a4fd-7d5cf83c8022" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278811} {:answer/id #uuid "3dbc014a-0203-582f-8cb4-ea9a4980b836" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279135} {:answer/id #uuid "5d71a5fd-a1a5-578b-b16d-463e3b9ca06e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279450} {:answer/id #uuid "8070acd2-0eb1-5b3d-8631-c79cba8f6cfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279743} {:answer/id #uuid "b07a55f4-358b-52f0-8ce8-2a341242e1f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280157}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18f5a574-0cca-57fa-a4fd-7d5cf83c8022" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278811} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fb211f7-a024-5d85-9487-56111b032805" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281001}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "636e23a3-a873-5229-81ee-5d1161e02dcd" :question/math-academy-id "q-163070" :question/problem "![](images/fd/fd1a5a140c3a2d430312d6f6ceeb21d6b7282119c8ec53365553ff845d8c64d5.png)\n\nFind the average value of the function $f(x,y)=x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ on the semicircle shown above." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that \n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\pi \\cdot (\\frac{1}{2})^{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{8}.\n$$\n\nThe average value of the function over a region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{\\text{Area}(D)}\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $\\frac{1}{2}$ centered at \n$(0,-\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $x=\\sqrt{-{y}^{2}-y}$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\sqrt{-{y}^{2}-y} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-{y}^{2}-y \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =-y \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) & =-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2} & =-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}+r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nTherefore, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 0}.\n$$\n\nUsing the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta [\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]_{0}^{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ((-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{4}-{0}^{4})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{4}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{[\\frac{{sin}^{5}⁡\\theta }{5}]}_{-\\pi /2}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}({sin}^{5}⁡(0)-{sin}^{5}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{(\\frac{\\pi }{8})}\\cdot \\frac{1}{20}=\\frac{2}{5\\pi }.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281590 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40766867-ebb7-5eae-98f1-229a23b4cdcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{e}^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279164} {:answer/id #uuid "4f5dff5d-c3e8-5c1d-8ed5-e22d38041a31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7{e}^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279314} {:answer/id #uuid "6c0a5784-1bfe-5f5c-8f30-c0f231bf0ac5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7{e}^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279571} {:answer/id #uuid "9866eeb7-e58b-579c-a22b-89643860465d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279937} {:answer/id #uuid "f819b7e9-c3d8-56b3-bfa4-f606feb0dd17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{e}^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280769}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f819b7e9-c3d8-56b3-bfa4-f606feb0dd17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{e}^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280769} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a3026a8-7c22-5511-8ffc-5378980efc8b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281056}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "76709b52-0813-5f66-abd5-aca7bde76362" :question/math-academy-id "q-163076" :question/problem "Consider the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{y},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf the tangent to $f$ at $a=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ is given by\n\n$$\nz-∗=\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix}(\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix})\n$$\n\nwhat is the sum of the missing values?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose a transformation $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{f}_{1}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{m}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${f}_{1},{f}_{2},…,{f}_{m}$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $f$ is differentiable at $x,$ then the total derivative of $f$ at $x,$ denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{y})=\\frac{2{e}^{2x}}{y},\\, & \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{y})=-\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2{e}^{2x}}{y} & -\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating $f$ and its derivative at $x=a=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},$ we have\n\n$$\nf(a)=\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2},\\,{f}^{′}(a)=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2} & -\\frac{{e}^{2}}{4}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent to $f$ at $x=a$ is given by\n\n$$\nz-f(a)={f}^{′}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $f(a),$ ${f}^{′}(a),$ and $a$ in the equation above, we obtain\n\n$$\nz-\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2} & -\\frac{{e}^{2}}{4}\\end{bmatrix}(\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the missing values is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}+{e}^{2}+(-\\frac{{e}^{2}}{4})=\\frac{5{e}^{2}}{4}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281601 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a2159cd-ca1d-52a3-8158-711386d7dc3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{y} & {e}^{x-y} \\\\ -\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}} & -{e}^{x-y}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278824} {:answer/id #uuid "265096a0-323d-5747-bf84-d4b8bad05502" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}} \\\\ -y{e}^{x-y} & x{e}^{x-y}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278932} {:answer/id #uuid "2d50d60a-ea1f-5bd9-a908-51ae26d6e264" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{y} & \\frac{x}{{y}^{2}} \\\\ -{e}^{x-y} & {e}^{x-y}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278990} {:answer/id #uuid "45213a92-906d-5fea-858d-97190bb5ed5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{y} & -\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}} \\\\ {e}^{x-y} & -{e}^{x-y}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279207} {:answer/id #uuid "65338b33-5b54-5452-be8c-4b4d0c1eedfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{y} & \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}} \\\\ {e}^{x-y} & -{e}^{x-y}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279510}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45213a92-906d-5fea-858d-97190bb5ed5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{y} & -\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}} \\\\ {e}^{x-y} & -{e}^{x-y}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279207} :answer-field/id #uuid "7cc55c89-7ca3-5217-803e-3b345111a52c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281061}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "754cc616-7c79-55a6-8b43-74dd54ff339a" :question/math-academy-id "q-163099" :question/problem "For the transformation $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2},$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{x}{y} \\\\ {e}^{x-y}\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\ncalculate ${f}^{′}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose a transformation $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{f}_{1}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{m}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${f}_{1},{f}_{2},…,{f}_{m}$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $f$ is differentiable at $x,$ then the total derivative of $f$ at $x,$ denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x}{y})=\\frac{1}{y},\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{x}{y})=-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x-y})={e}^{x-y},\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x-y})=-{e}^{x-y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{y} & -\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}} \\\\ {e}^{x-y} & -{e}^{x-y}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281604 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a163f00-423b-57e8-ae46-b60f111d48a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278964} {:answer/id #uuid "3582f22b-3311-5a03-87b8-3529a0043ed8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279060} {:answer/id #uuid "4bc1b6c6-2219-5b6b-b6fb-06d4eeaed11a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279269} {:answer/id #uuid "7be2b338-9c19-55aa-9af1-1128dd120960" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279698} {:answer/id #uuid "dd278181-66cd-5b8a-9eb0-5c8613158328" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280542}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a163f00-423b-57e8-ae46-b60f111d48a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278964} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c6589ef-e870-5ba4-8f44-69c9d5eac8cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280992}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "46cd9f15-1dee-5aee-b3d9-70903f558ae8" :question/math-academy-id "q-163111" :question/problem "For the function of two variables \n$f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y+1),$\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=ax-y-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+bxy+c{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" :question/worked-solution "If the function $f(x,y)$ is twice differentiable at $(x,y)=(0,0),$ then the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈\\overbrace{f(0,0)+{f}_{x}(0,0)\\cdot x+{f}_{y}(0,0)\\cdot y}^{\\text{Linear approximation}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+\\underbrace{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(0,0)\\cdot {x}^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(0,0)\\cdot xy+{f}_{yy}(0,0)\\cdot {y}^{2})}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial.\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{x-y+1}, & \\,{f}_{y}(x,y) & =-\\frac{1}{x-y+1} \\\\ {f}_{x}(0,0) & =1, & \\,{f}_{y}(0,0) & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(x,y) & =-\\frac{1}{(x-y+1)^{2}}, & \\,{f}_{xx}(0,0) & =-1 \\\\ {f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{(x-y+1)^{2}}, & \\,{f}_{xy}(0,0) & =1 \\\\ {f}_{yy}(x,y) & =-\\frac{1}{(x-y+1)^{2}}, & \\,{f}_{yy}(0,0) & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈0+1\\cdot x+(-1)\\cdot y+\\frac{1}{2}(-1\\cdot {x}^{2}+2(1)\\cdot xy+(-1)\\cdot {y}^{2}) \\\\  & =1x-y-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+1xy+(-\\frac{1}{2}){y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=1+1+(-\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281607 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0488d7ff-b23d-57ca-ac1f-3b003b1d4cad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278628} {:answer/id #uuid "19abce14-7fbc-5539-b049-f6588065cc58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278820} {:answer/id #uuid "1b20a70e-e52e-59a8-a425-efc1714f3b05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278843} {:answer/id #uuid "4be49a5e-04bf-5a10-ba8f-0ff99825ca37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279271} {:answer/id #uuid "f7c391e8-39ad-5fb2-9766-ec1eb8cec68c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280763}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19abce14-7fbc-5539-b049-f6588065cc58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278820} :answer-field/id #uuid "11825cc6-3e2c-51ee-8c64-1a71cc1b22e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280861}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e7670cca-4082-532e-925a-af41e094c0c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-163122" :question/problem "For the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\n$$\n\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}∗ \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}∗ \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind the sum of the three missing expressions." :question/worked-solution "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(a)={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}, & \\,{f}^{″}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the first derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & \\operatorname{sin}⁡x-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & \\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y & -x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}x-\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x-\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the sum of the missing expressions is\n\n$$\n(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})+(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})+(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=2x-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281612 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39e916b0-4053-558e-b9e2-fb010d0079e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279101} {:answer/id #uuid "6717d5eb-a675-55ba-ab48-3b932471e1a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279525} {:answer/id #uuid "67a4e5bc-094b-5bc0-b24d-26314e5a86e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279532} {:answer/id #uuid "e735fad1-0d3b-5a5c-9df6-04260dc4278f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280629} {:answer/id #uuid "f9a3f08e-da67-596e-a0f8-03c03a5fd812" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280784}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6717d5eb-a675-55ba-ab48-3b932471e1a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279525} :answer-field/id #uuid "52a7cb95-9f50-5d35-89f8-e9b2f23fadd9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280969}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "51a515de-4536-5de9-a9e1-b88421eacc3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-163148" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)$ and the region $D$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=6{x}^{2}-2y+2,\\,D={(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that the volume of the solid enclosed between the surface $z=f(x,y)$ and the $xy$-plane over the region $D$ equals the volume of the right cylinder with base $D$ and height $f(a,b).$ Find $b$ given that $a=\\frac{1}{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A=f(a,b)\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral on the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}(6{x}^{2}-2y+2)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(6{x}^{2}-2y+2)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}[6{x}^{2}y-{y}^{2}+2y]_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(6{x}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[2{x}^{3}+x]_{x=-1}^{x=1} \\\\  & =(2\\cdot {1}^{3}+1)-(2\\cdot (-1)^{3}+(-1)) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=1\\cdot 2=2.\n$$\n\nAlso, we are told that $a=\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, our initial equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6 & =f(\\frac{1}{2},b)\\cdot 2 \\\\ 6 & =(6\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-2b+2)\\cdot 2 \\\\ 3 & =\\frac{7}{2}-2b \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & =-2b \\\\ b & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4})\\in D.$ Therefore, the answer is $b=\\frac{1}{4}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281619 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16205635-c6e2-54cc-b321-158a614700bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278788} {:answer/id #uuid "40f6af20-b691-5a75-9ab5-3967bde5cf92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279169} {:answer/id #uuid "92070dc4-c11e-5c9f-9a77-1e1f7a591dd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279892} {:answer/id #uuid "af97bdd4-d844-5c5e-ab36-f279e9a4dc11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280146} {:answer/id #uuid "bd69ec15-2dd8-58af-9b35-578926bf94d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280256}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af97bdd4-d844-5c5e-ab36-f279e9a4dc11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280146} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1ded9e2-7680-5857-96a0-f9ff21c5ecfb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281261}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "53646bcf-6387-54da-9ed9-6c682d87bd18" :question/math-academy-id "q-163165" :question/problem "Consider the lamina that occupies the region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{x}}$ and has the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)=y.$ Given that the mass of the lamina is $\\frac{9}{4}$ units and its center of mass is located at $(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y}),$ what is the value of $\\overset{―}{x}?$" :question/worked-solution "The center of mass of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)$ is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y})=(\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m},\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $m={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A$ is the mass of the lamina,\n\n- ${M}_{x}={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\\,$ is the moment of the lamina about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- ${M}_{y}={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A$ is the moment of the lamina about the $y$-axis.\n\nSince we require the $x$-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the $y$-axis: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\cdot y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{x}}xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}x[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{x}}y\\,\\text{d}y]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}x{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}x(\\frac{x}{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}]}_{x=0}^{x=3} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m}=\\frac{(\\frac{9}{2})}{(\\frac{9}{4})}=2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281652 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03807599-de78-5ad7-a7e9-4c7bb13ddb41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278614} {:answer/id #uuid "6c4be14e-90f4-58b3-9526-ee12c5821967" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279575} {:answer/id #uuid "86a4b742-abbd-5e07-b5fb-d64388299e8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279794} {:answer/id #uuid "c659f522-be42-55a2-8ae8-cad12771bb04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280330} {:answer/id #uuid "dccc430d-d0d0-5a02-ad45-9f948009eaa1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280541}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6c4be14e-90f4-58b3-9526-ee12c5821967" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279575} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1213e4f-6a9b-5d11-ae9d-60df7a2d94b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281117}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1ba106c5-525d-577c-b0dd-66ad242d632a" :question/math-academy-id "q-163255" :question/problem "Find the average value of the function $f(x,y)=12x{y}^{3}$ on the region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{x}{4}}$ given that the area of the region is $\\frac{1}{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Applying the average value formula, we find that the average value of the function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{ave}} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{Area}(D)}\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{2})}{∬}_{D}12x{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{x/4}12x{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}[3x{y}^{4}]_{y=0}^{y=x/4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{3{x}^{5}}{256}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2[\\frac{{x}^{6}}{512}]_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =2(\\frac{1}{8}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281662 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27060319-319a-5c93-87a4-021b464e69b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278938} {:answer/id #uuid "96cddbff-aa96-5c15-b451-85bc3b5899ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279926} {:answer/id #uuid "d6297845-0a0a-51e3-9056-3d94de569441" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280482} {:answer/id #uuid "d932caf8-3377-5328-b82d-2c72ffe1d077" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280511} {:answer/id #uuid "fb6871d0-cf0d-5786-9a5c-fd24e0463ae0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{5\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280794}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb6871d0-cf0d-5786-9a5c-fd24e0463ae0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{5\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280794} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4bf3c23-4651-531a-8ea1-05778a87081e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281209}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4da84d34-65b6-5264-9689-a8f568b0485b" :question/math-academy-id "q-163278" :question/problem "![](images/f8/f84a8fcd4ed668a6b94d5b084ade49dc8fc027feff4baea862e2feaf68221534.png)\n\nFind the average value of the function $f(x,y)=y\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ on the semicircle shown above." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that \n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\pi \\cdot {1}^{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nThe average value of the function over a region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{\\text{Area}(D)}\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $1$ centered at \n$(-1,0).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $y=\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}-2x}$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}-2x} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-{x}^{2}-2x \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =-2x \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =-2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) & =-2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2} & =-2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}+2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nTherefore, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}y\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta [\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]_{0}^{-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ((-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{4}-{0}^{4})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }16\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{4}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{[-\\frac{{cos}^{5}⁡\\theta }{5}]}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{5}({cos}^{5}⁡\\pi -{cos}^{5}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}\\cdot \\frac{4}{5}=\\frac{8}{5\\pi }.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281663 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02228a55-f0cd-5d20-a57e-0a6be96e6db4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278600} {:answer/id #uuid "20802c9f-44b7-54c4-98fc-e21f7cad1d65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278891} {:answer/id #uuid "2f618d22-048b-597a-97ca-eb05ca9ee64e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279004} {:answer/id #uuid "34a613ef-9f35-5a47-9476-0c26e7c87ec4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279046} {:answer/id #uuid "d964673e-ff68-55cd-ae5e-82c200ba663a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280512}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d964673e-ff68-55cd-ae5e-82c200ba663a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280512} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b55bdf5-772a-5e7e-b2a0-07c63cb6c150" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281089}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "128ce1a7-9b5d-51fb-9a3d-78ee6a5214c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-163279" :question/problem "![](images/71/719f86db69489f62bcbe2753e88cdc6713f04dc6f53415cc2484e2eaab8ffac3.png)\n\nFind the average value of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{x}{y}$ on the semicircle shown above." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that \n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\pi \\cdot {1}^{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nThe average value of the function over a region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{\\text{Area}(D)}\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $1$ centered at \n$(0,1).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $x=\\sqrt{2y-{y}^{2}}$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\sqrt{2y-{y}^{2}} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =2y-{y}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =2y \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) & =2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2} & =2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nTherefore, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{y}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{[\\frac{{r}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }(2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -0)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta ]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(-1-1) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}\\cdot 1=\\frac{2}{\\pi }.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281664 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0007cf9c-0395-5509-b616-4be695ec13cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278584} {:answer/id #uuid "2f6a9d20-68f0-5b58-918b-8ef34081db13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279005} {:answer/id #uuid "842bc963-2d06-5322-bb34-77f3b33fea49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279777} {:answer/id #uuid "a8fdd69b-b6ba-5d0d-9e9d-54a97ec7d638" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280090} {:answer/id #uuid "b466adf4-9e43-5073-97cf-93330bff4141" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280181}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f6a9d20-68f0-5b58-918b-8ef34081db13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279005} :answer-field/id #uuid "14124592-bf34-56d3-a109-e9e7b9325d95" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280866}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "319f1b6f-8e13-5060-954e-d52a8b4d9a60" :question/math-academy-id "q-163280" :question/problem "Consider a thin plate $D$ that has the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)={x}^{3}$ and is enclosed between the curve $y=x$ and the $x$-axis for $0\\le x\\le 2.$ Given that the mass of the plate is $\\frac{32}{5}$ units and its center of mass is located at $(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y}),$ what is the value of $\\overset{―}{y}?$" :question/worked-solution "The center of mass of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)$ is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y})=(\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m},\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $m={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A$ is the mass of the lamina,\n\n- ${M}_{x}={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\\,$ is the moment of the lamina about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- ${M}_{y}={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A$ is the moment of the lamina about the $y$-axis.\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type I plane region:\n\n![](images/a0/a042919f73d668cac20ead2752c689e57880f60bc096747f41cec3aeb3ed62ca.png)\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le x}\n$$\n\nand $\\lambda (x,y)={x}^{3}.$ \n\nSince we require the $y$-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the $x$-axis: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}y{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{3}[{\\int }_{0}^{x}y\\,\\text{d}y]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{3}\\cdot {[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{12}{x}^{6}]}_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot 64 \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}=\\frac{(\\frac{16}{3})}{(\\frac{32}{5})}=\\frac{5}{6}.\n$$"}]
